10516	9186	0	Well one approach could be to use machine learning and train with hand histories (a lot of them!). Your classifications (the bots decision) would be fold, call/check and raise. This approach isn't great because it assumes fixed raise and call sizes. You could refine further though and have classifications for half pot bets and full pot bets, etc. You want the bot to pick the classification that has the highest EV as defined in the hand histories, but also the closest action in the present moment to the hand history.	2017-08-02 23:06:22.247	user4555	\N
10517	9186	0	So you would have a "closest" function applied to the current action multiplied by the EVs of each decision. The highest number wins and the bot picks that option. This is just one way to do it and it would require a massive number of hand histories to be any good. The other option is to create a bot that actually calculates probabilities. The usual case in computing is that some sort of compromise between the two is best for performance.	2017-08-02 23:12:51.56	user4555	\N
