11996	10092	0	It's about identifying the opponent's hand. If you ask yourself ahead of time, will I call three streets of bets, and the answer is YES, then you might as well reraise on flop (which is cheaper than calling three pot size bets). The idea is to polarize the range so much that you can comfortably fold. If you reraise and he goes over the top or calls you, you virtually KNOW you are beat, you can fold easily.\n\nLeading out achieve the same thing but with less certainty. if you check-raise someone, they are unlikely to call you with a bluff (unless they are Tom Dwan or something) or a weak hand.	2018-08-31 15:37:18.607	\N	1896
11997	10092	0	So to clarify, leading out is also ok, but it loses the value of "catching a c-bet bluff". If you know your opponent is likely to c-bet with any flop, check-reraise to catch a bluff is a good move. If he calls or goes over, it's not a bluff, if he folds, he's weak or bluffing and you get one extra bet out of him. So it really depends on how often you think he will c-bet with air in that situation.	2018-08-31 15:39:47.153	\N	1896
