12001	10092	0	Call the flop makes I think makes you harder to play.  If you only raise medium strength hands then you can get 3 bet and pushed off the pot.	2018-08-31 20:14:22.643	\N	3876
12002	10092	0	That's 100% the same logic with any action if you do it all the time. Simply don't do it all the time. Reraising is to polarize his range, so your decision can be easier afterwards. There's always a chance someone can dry bluff reraise you off a medium strength hand with air, but in reality, it doesn't happen often enough to be a real consideration. You can't win every situation, but you want to at least win the "common situation". Commonly if you reraise a pot and gets called, he beats medium pair, and that's good enough mathematically to make it a +EV play.	2018-08-31 20:20:39.897	\N	1896
12003	10097	0	In this instance I am calling a monster AA, KK, or AK preflop. As she had two limp before her, and based on some hours of playing with her, I’d have expected her to raise the blinds long before it got to me. The reraise was worrying, but it reeked of her standing up to me for reasons I can’t recall anymore. Then she checked the flop. Once again, it reeked of a bluff/semi-bluff.	2018-08-31 20:20:52.807	\N	6311
12004	10092	0	Calling does have its advantages, but like I said it depends on your goal and read. If you feel the villain will fire out no matter what (with any cards), then check-call is the right move because you will catch enough bluffs to make your losing hands "worth it". If you feel like 75% of the time, you get triple barrel bluffs, then check-calling is definitely worth it. But if you aren't sure what you want to do, you need to reraise to make your future decisions easier (polarize your opponent's range).	2018-08-31 20:23:30.023	\N	1896
