31	2	13	43de57c7-c56e-47ab-93cd-eb10a307382f	2012-01-10 19:29:42.743	12	\N	\N	To answer your specific example, if there's a flush on the table (say 3, 4, 5, 6, A spades), and the two players have no over spade (a spade higher than the 6 on the table), and there is no straight flush (no one has the 2 of spades), then they split the pot.\r\n\r\nIf they both have 7s, per your example, they are meaningless if they aren't spades.  If one has a 7 of spades, though, then they have the *higher* flush (A/7 high, vs. A/6 high), and win the whole pot.\r\n\r\nEven if the player with the 2 has a spades, it is lower in value than all other cards on the table flush, and therefore does not play (the value of each player's hand is determined by the best combination of 5 cards between their hand and the table).
