849	2	346	c6fd2bfd-3f4d-444c-9992-d54e0a70dded	2012-01-27 04:02:37.683	17	\N	\N	A redraw generally tends to mean that you have the best hand, and you have a draw to another bettwe hand.  In Texas Hold 'Em, the best example would be a set on the flop.  If you get all-in against a flush draw, even though they have a draw to a better hand (a flush), you have a re-draw to a hand that can beat their draw (a full house).  The term is more useful in an Omaha type game, where 2 players can frequently hold the same hand (e.g. a nut straight), but one can have a redraw to a nut flush or full house.
