1367	2	561	48c06c87-fea3-4ee7-99af-eff85628f718	2012-03-28 11:13:54.74	341	\N	\N	He cannot fold until it's his turn, so his raise must be met by anyone who wants to remain active.  When it is his turn to bet and he folds by default, his hand is dead and loses all interest in the pot.  If no one remains to take the pot, either play a round of Straight Poker to determine the winner, or leave it in to start the next pot.
1161	2	473	aab90764-b75e-443d-a14b-4f9b8b936ac7	2012-02-22 22:49:08.787	56	\N	\N	This question may go Wiki at some point. \r\n\r\nFrom the ones I've read, I'd recommend:\r\n\r\n  - [Every Hand Revealed - Gus Hansen][1] - from a pro who managed to win this one. You see it all, good moves and bad.\r\n  - Harrington on Holdem ([Vol 1][2] and [Vol 2][3]) - The basics, and not so basics, of Holdem. A necessary start to get your M moving and your EV in line.\r\n  - [Power Hold'em Strategy][4] - I bought this for Daniel Negreanu's section on lowball. Still working my way through it.\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/0818407271/ref=oh_o00_s00_i00_details\r\n  [2]: https://www.amazon.com/gp/css/order-history/ref=ohs_order_orderid?ie=UTF8&opt=ab&qid=&search=103-2273811-9147852&sr=\r\n  [3]: https://www.amazon.com/gp/css/order-history/ref=ohs_order_orderid?ie=UTF8&opt=ab&qid=&search=103-2788717-8379038&sr=\r\n  [4]: http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1580422047/ref=oh_o01_s00_i01_details
1162	2	474	fa5d31e9-4697-457c-a81e-ebe27cdc6e95	2012-02-23 02:39:25.323	22	\N	\N	The big four are definitely:\r\n\r\n - [Theory of Poker](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1880685000?ie=UTF8&tag=grinderschool-20&linkCode=as2&camp=1789&creative=9325&creativeASIN=1880685000) by David Sklansky - perhaps the best book for establishing an understanding of poker theory in general. This one is worthwhile no matter what poker game you're into.\r\n - [Super System](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1880685000?ie=UTF8&tag=grinderschool-20&linkCode=as2&camp=1789&creative=9325&creativeASIN=1880685000) compiled by Doyle Brunson - between this and its sequel, you can get a good ground-level understanding of any game. It's not something to read cover to cover, but it's a resource you should have and refer to any time you move into a new game.\r\n - [No Limit Hold'em Theory & Practice](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1880685000?ie=UTF8&tag=grinderschool-20&linkCode=as2&camp=1789&creative=9325&creativeASIN=1880685000) by David Sklansky and Ed Miller - gives an all round explanation of no limit hold'em cash games.\r\n - [Small Stakes Hold'em](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1880685000?ie=UTF8&tag=grinderschool-20&linkCode=as2&camp=1789&creative=9325&creativeASIN=1880685000) by David Sklansky - it's a book based on limit, but the theory can be applied to no limit just as easily. This was one of the go-to books on hold'em before the poker explosion happened.\r\n\r\nI'd throw the Harrington on Hold'em books in there as well for No Limit Hold'em tournaments, but Al G already mentioned them, so there's no need for repetition.
