1411	2	575	77aa2bcb-7d9d-4599-bed0-b8bc398d7ccf	2012-04-08 14:16:56.66	22	\N	\N	I think that they are using the term "semi-bluff" pretty loosely here. As I read it, I take it to be due to the fact that you still have a fair amount of equity against most of your opponents' calling range. \r\n\r\nOften you actually have better equity with junk than with mediocre high-card hands, since 7&clubs;2&diamond; will probably not share any cards with your opponents, but they may well hold a Q, making Q&hearts;8&hearts; a disaster when you hit a Q. \r\n\r\nThe 7&clubs;2&diamond; is a "semi-bluff" because it has a better possibility of being good when it hits post-flop. It still seems like a stretch to me to call it a "semi-bluff" but I believe that's the logic behind the statement.
1413	5	574	945e5db0-8b21-4213-b4cd-b7eeb74757d2	2012-04-09 00:34:57.833	30	\N	Detailed the answer a bit more	Because the 555 are not just 555, it's a full house (fives full of queens). Elezra only has QQQ.\r\n\r\nIn Hold'em you have to make the best five cards out of seven: using either 0, 1 or 2 of your holecards and either 3, 4 or 5 community cards.\r\n\r\nIn the deal you linked to the community cards are:\r\n\r\n3&diamond;5&spades;Q&spades;Q&clubs;J&hearts;\r\n\r\nThe best hand Eli Elezra can make with his Q&diamond;2&hearts; is:\r\n\r\nQ&spades;Q&clubs;Q&diamond;J&hearts;5&spades;   (a set of Queens)\r\n\r\nwhile the best hand Doyle Brunson can make with his 5&diamond;5&clubs; is:\r\n\r\n5&spades;5&diamond;5&clubs;Q&spades;Q&clubs; (a full house, Fives full of Queens)\r\n\r\n \r\n
