3118	2	1221	6e061d64-b1a8-47be-b27d-d6e35799693e	2013-03-18 20:20:06.013	833	\N	\N	Poker is a game of skill. The trick is to play each hand correctly. Bad hands like 72-offsuit are best played by folding. Other hands like AA are best played by raising. Hands in the middle are payed best by considering the value of the hand and your position at the table.
3119	2	1222	7d24a290-fb6b-4665-a6c3-a0936958918a	2013-03-18 22:00:16.07	819	\N	\N	You're missing the whole betting process, which is key in poker.  Cards are dealt randomly, but afterwards, the players will wager on the strength of their hand until one of two things happen:\r\n\r\n- all players but one have folded their hands or; \r\n\r\n- the latest bet have been called by everyone still at the table.\r\n\r\nSo if you are a winning poker player, your betting will be such that:\r\n\r\n- players holding worse hands than yours will continue and /or,\r\n\r\n- players holding better hands than your will fold. \r\n\r\nIn other words, a lot of hands will never go to showdown and when they do, it is not rare that the player who wins the pot is not the one who had the best hand to start with.
3120	2	1223	3e204767-26f9-40f4-a0ec-a0233e9ed318	2013-03-19 01:43:55.457	828	\N	\N	The way poker games are structured is such that under normal circumstances there is an element of luck combined with an element of skill. Of course the cards you use to make your hand are completely random (in hold'em), and therefore this is the element of luck. But the betting structure involves calculating pot odds, psychology, and reading people. REalize though that certain structures have more luck than others. For example, suppose you are playing no-limit and someone has a flip coin type strategy for going all in, or folding. This removes almost all of the skill in the game, even against the top pros. Though there are some skillful tactics you can use to beat this strategy, if the blinds are small enough compared to the stack size. However, in limit poker, when playing a flip coin type strategy against a pro with a significant stack size, (perhaps 100 big blinds), the pro will likely win, I would guess 90% of the time or higher (in a freeze out setting). If this is true then you might have to admit that it's a skill game.
3125	2	1226	e7666468-cdc1-49e9-b91b-51a58543f2e3	2013-03-19 09:33:11.08	792	\N	\N	9 player cash game. Blinds 1c/2c. I am UTG with K:hearts: K:diamonds: I raise to 8c. Everyone folds to the button who calls. SB and BB. The pot is 16c. Flop comes 9:spades: 2:spades: K:clubs:. The button is sort of a calling station (calls pot-sized bets with gutshot draws!). I bet 16c. He calls. Turn is 4:diamonds:. I bet 48c into a 48c pot. He calls again! The pot is now $1.44. River comes Q:spades:\r\n\r\nQuestion1. Should I bet? If I don't bet, he might try to bluff me. And then I wouldn't know whether to call. If I should bet, how much should I bet. (my remaining stack is around $4, his is around $3)\r\n\r\nQuestion2. I did bet 75c. Perhaps he hit his draw or sensed weakness, he raised to $2. Should I call?\r\n\r\nGenerally, when you're out of position and feel that your opponent wrongly calls you with a draw, should you continue betting when one of the possible draws completes?  
