3260	2	1273	3a6ad07e-3d2f-4cd3-b2a8-e545d293eee5	2013-04-07 18:25:44	859	\N	\N	At first, rank, as you define it, has nothing to do with the stages of a tournament. It has to do with a thing, called the M factor. M represents the number of orbits that you will survive if you never play a hand, if you fold immediately, when your chance is given. Its formula is:\r\n\r\n**M=(Stack size)/(blinds+antes)**\r\n\r\nfor a full (10-man) table.\r\n\r\nHow does M affect how you play? The larger the **M** is, the closer to "normal poker" you play, because you have lots of space for maneuvers. The smaller **M** you have, the closer to mechanical poker you will play. When **M** is low enough (5 or less), you have a simple problem: push or fold preflop.\r\n\r\nFrom this theory, as long as the M increases, the play will, until a point, become looser because there are bigger pots to be won and you need to win to survive. This is probably why you think that there is a lot of bluffing. It may be, but surely there is a strategy behind it.\r\n\r\nThe reason for your position declines wile advancing the tournament is that you probably keep playing tight for longer than necessary. The others gather pots, you don't.\r\n\r\nOne last thing: you mentioned the 24x7 freerolls. As this is a **freeroll**, expect the play to be more chaotic, because there isn't any stakes for people to care. But when you play tournaments with buy-in of at least 1$, people apply some theory.\r\n\r\nHope it helps,\r\nGood luck!
