3367	2	1309	dfee8c09-ed98-4ac8-ac3f-ba4044b14cb2	2013-04-24 13:04:20.607	694	\N	\N	I haven't heard the term used before, so I created my own definition of it:\r\n\r\n> Short stack syndrome - the extreme measures a\r\n> short-stacked player will take to stay in the game.\r\n\r\nThe characteristics of this effect on a player tends to include higher aggression - constant raises or all-in's most hands and as most of them have suffered earlier on in the game at some point (either by being donked out or by making a big mistake themselves) they're completely on tilt and behaving irrationally.\r\n\r\nIf you're a larger/leading stack, you don't tend to want to take the chance and call the bluff of these kinds of players in case they luck out and you lose your lead over something like that - then again, you don't want to keep folding and letting them take the blinds.\r\n\r\nSo, I guess my question is, what is the best way to deal with short stack syndrome players online? (In my experience, this tends to be more of an online problem as opposed to real life poker).
