3594	2	1415	d7fd7135-aae9-4cd3-b2d0-3ff011ae6ed5	2013-06-24 08:40:45.94	417	\N	\N	Single stand alone tell is really not enough to get any good advantage.\r\n\r\nYou need to observe, if the player you are focused on try to act and bluff you, or not. Many players just try to hide their emotions and do not act, while many others try to signal tells that are opposite to their hand strength.\r\n\r\n[Caro's book][1] of poker tells is really great source of information about this topic.\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://www.amazon.com/Caros-Book-Poker-Tells-Psychology/dp/1580420826
3595	2	1416	f2e22c19-0259-4b6b-bec2-ae14c416d9ea	2013-06-24 09:39:04.78	964	\N	\N	Chen's formula is limited in the way that any formula is - as said above it doesn't take account of who you're playing against. Also, though, it has major flaws in logic (why under connectedness would Ace not count as high or low? Why would you get a bonus for making a straight with community cards higher than your hole cards, but not with community lower than your hole cards - that's just asking to get beaten by a higher straight).\r\nIn some ways, at least a formula gets beginners thinking about the strength of their hand, but the point of the formula is supposed to be to make things easier - yet the beginner still has to...\r\n\r\na) Calculate the Chen rating \r\n    \r\nb) Decide on where the line between fold & play is, taking into account...\r\n\r\n 2. position\r\n 3. number of players at the table\r\n 4. size of the blinds compared to size of stack (tournament)\r\n\r\nI guess there's a place for a formula like  this, but it needs to be better thought out - and preferably backed up by some stats.\r\n
3597	2	1417	98ed1fec-a0e2-4898-9051-e6d6aaab3f36	2013-06-29 06:03:37.52	973	\N	\N	I'm a newbie in Poker and so I would like you to judge the rightness of my actions on the following hand:\r\n\r\nYesterday I was playing hold'em NL and my cards were a premium hand, K♣ K♦. There were eight in the table besides me. I was in the worst position, Under the Gun. For the reason of the premium hand I raised the Big Blind three times. The first three after me folded, but the fourth re-raised two times and the fifth and sixth called. The Small and Big Blind called too. To show aggression I re-re-raised three times. The two blinds folded this time, but the fourth, fifth and sixth called.\r\n\r\nThe flop came 3♣,5♦,6♥. Then I had just a pair of Kings. Despite the impossibility of a flush, everyone with a 2,4 or 4,7 could perform a straight. This time I called to see other's reactions (Should I have given raise?). The fourth called, the fifth folded and the sixth called too. The turn came 5♣. Anyone with a 5 would have Three of a kind and then I would be smashed unless the gods helped me on the river with another K. I decided to just call. The fourth raised four times and the sixth folded. So I was left with a difficult question: to fold for fourth's action or not?\r\n\r\nI called. The river was: A♥. This didn't help me, on the contrary, made my situation even worse, because any other A would form a pair of aces. However, to pretend that I was in the best with Three of a kind of Aces I went All-in, hoping him to be scared and fold. The villain called and so the showdown was:\r\n\r\n\r\nBoard cards: 3♣,5♦,6♥ 5♣ A♥\r\n\r\nMy hand: K♣K♦\r\n\r\nOpponent's hand: K♦2♦\r\n\r\n\r\nI won with a pair of Kings, however, what I did was total gambling. My actions were correct? If not, what should I have done? Should I have given up when that 5 appeared on the turn and the villain was pretending Three of a kind?
