3979	2	1577	70ae5693-5810-46ca-b408-41ce21cd44b8	2013-10-14 14:20:08.33	674	\N	\N	There are `50 × 49 ÷ 2 = 1,225` possible starting hands that can be dealt preflop to an opponent when you are heads up.  (We divide by two previously because the order that the cards are dealt is irrelevant.) \r\n\r\nIn the situation that you have described above, he can have two of those starting hands, so the odds of a randomly dealt hand being one of those two would be 2 out of 1,225, which is 1:612, or 0.16% of the time.\r\n\r\nI you have two opponents, the odds for the second opponent would be calculated in a similar manner, and you add the two together to determine the odds of either opponent having the hand.
3982	2	1580	72b53aad-3cb1-46f8-8d5e-f454b5e16f6c	2013-10-15 12:24:24.123	1110	\N	\N	The maths is wrong... It should be 3 out of 1225 not 2 because 2d6d is possible as well as Ad2d and 6d7d.\r\n\r\nBut anyway the answer would be wrong for a real game - the answer would assume that the opponent had random cards, which isn't true in reality. It would depend on how the hand played out as to how likely an opponent is to have those cards. For example if it is 3-bet preflop hands like 6d7d are less likely; if it is a limped pot preflop they are more likely.
3983	5	1577	efdf9c8b-f360-43fa-824f-a20fdc51029f	2013-10-15 21:34:26.963	674	\N	Updated math for a hand that I forgot.	There are `50 × 49 ÷ 2 = 1,225` possible starting hands that can be dealt preflop to an opponent when you are heads up.  (We divide by two previously because the order that the cards are dealt is irrelevant.) \r\n\r\nIn the situation that you have described above, he can have two of those starting hands, so the odds of a randomly dealt hand being one of those two would be 3 out of 1,225, which is 1:407, or 0.25% of the time.\r\n\r\nI you have two opponents, the odds for the second opponent would be calculated in a similar manner, and you add the two together to determine the odds of either opponent having the hand.
