5590	2	2823	1dfb03de-89c1-4150-98a1-9f732b32aa2d	2014-03-22 20:18:30.097	30	\N	\N	Odds of 4:1 corresponds to a probability *p* = 0.20.\r\n\r\nThe article talks about what to expect if you make the call four times.\r\n\r\nWell, if you play four deals like this, there's still a p = 0.4096 (40.96%) that you lose the deal all four times (0.8 exp 4 gives 0.4096).\r\n\r\nSo you only have (1 - p), which gives 0.5904 (59.04%) chance you'll win *at least* one of the four deals.\r\n\r\nNow it is correct to say that you should always call when you have explicit odds in your favor: this is immensely EV+. (let alone the implied odds and discussions about fold equity if *you* are the one pushing all in semi-bluffing your draws)\r\n\r\nBut it is not correct to say that *"If you make this call four times, mathematics says that you will hit your draw once"*. That's not what probabilities says.\r\n\r\nProbabilities says that with p = 0.20 and four runs you have:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% chance to lose all four deals\r\n- 40.96% chance to win one the four deals\r\n- 15.36% chance to win two of the four deals\r\n-  2.56% chance to win three of the four deals\r\n-  0.16% chance to win all four deals\r\n\r\nTo verify this, I just wrote a "game": it is either paying you 5 "units" when the random number is between [0..19] inclusive or removing you 1 unit when the random number is between [20.99] inclusive.\r\n\r\nI then ran 20 series of 4 "deals". Here are the results of a sample run:\r\n\r\n [2 2 -4 2 -4 2 2 -4 -4 2 8 8 -4 2 -4 2 2 8 -4 -4]\r\n\r\nAs you can see, quite often you'll still lose the four deals: getting -4 because you lost four times 1 unit.\r\n\r\nI did verify the frequencies I computed above over 10 millions runs and got this (I did just fix the % while writing this answer):\r\n\r\n {-4 0.4096233%,  2 0.4098634%,  8 0.1533343%,  14 0.0255836%,  20 0.0015954%}\r\n\r\nFor what it's worth, here's the full (Clojure) code used to compute this: \r\n\r\n    (frequencies \r\n     (take 1000000\r\n           (repeatedly\r\n            #(apply +\r\n                    (for [x (range 4)]\r\n                      (if (< (rand-int 100) 20) 5 -1))))))\r\n\r\n
5591	5	2823	2b91bbdf-f5bb-46d8-9851-a4c8ceecb92a	2014-03-22 20:27:02.707	30	\N	added 481 characters in body	Odds of 4:1 corresponds to a probability *p* = 0.20.\r\n\r\nIf you check the Wikipedia article on odds, you'll see that it's correct to write it this way:\r\n\r\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odds\r\n\r\n> For example, a probability 0.20 is represented as "4 to 1 against"\r\n> (written as 4-1, 4:1, or 4/1), since there are five outcomes of which\r\n> four are unsuccessful.\r\n\r\nHowever the article you quoted is not correct (or badly worded). The article talks about what happens if you play four deals with 4:1 odds (doing an EV+ call) and says that you'll hit your draw once.\r\n\r\nWell, if you play four deals like this, there's still a p = 0.4096 (40.96%) that you lose the deal all four times (0.8 exp 4 gives 0.4096).\r\n\r\nSo you only have (1 - p), which gives 0.5904 (59.04%) chance you'll win *at least* one of the four deals.\r\n\r\nNow it is correct to say that you should always call when you have explicit odds in your favor: this is immensely EV+. (let alone the implied odds and discussions about fold equity if *you* are the one pushing all in semi-bluffing your draws)\r\n\r\nBut it is not correct to say that *"If you make this call four times, mathematics says that you will hit your draw once"*. That's not what probabilities says.\r\n\r\nProbabilities says that with p = 0.20 and four runs you have:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% chance to lose all four deals\r\n- 40.96% chance to win one the four deals\r\n- 15.36% chance to win two of the four deals\r\n-  2.56% chance to win three of the four deals\r\n-  0.16% chance to win all four deals\r\n\r\nTo verify this, I just wrote a "game": it is either paying you 5 "units" when the random number is between [0..19] inclusive or removing you 1 unit when the random number is between [20.99] inclusive.\r\n\r\nI then ran 20 series of 4 "deals". Here are the results of a sample run:\r\n\r\n [2 2 -4 2 -4 2 2 -4 -4 2 8 8 -4 2 -4 2 2 8 -4 -4]\r\n\r\nAs you can see, quite often you'll still lose the four deals: getting -4 because you lost four times 1 unit.\r\n\r\nI did verify the frequencies I computed above over 10 millions runs and got this (I did just fix the % while writing this answer):\r\n\r\n {-4 0.4096233%,  2 0.4098634%,  8 0.1533343%,  14 0.0255836%,  20 0.0015954%}\r\n\r\nSo if you run four deals:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% of the time you'll lose four units (lose four times)\r\n- 40.96% of the time you'll win 2 units (lose 1 three times, win 5 once)\r\n- 15.36% of the time you'll win 8 units (lose 2 two times, win 5 twice)\r\n-  2.56% of the time you'll win 14 units (lose 1 once, win 5 three times)\r\n-  0.16% of the time you'll win 20 units (win four times)\r\n\r\nFor what it's worth, here's the full (Clojure) code used to verify these numbers: \r\n\r\n    (frequencies \r\n     (take 1000000\r\n           (repeatedly\r\n            #(apply +\r\n                    (for [x (range 4)]\r\n                      (if (< (rand-int 100) 20) 5 -1))))))\r\n\r\nNow of course where I agree with the article is that it is EV+ to call getting 5:1 on your call when you're 4:1 to hit the winning hand.\r\n
