5598	5	2823	25e48d04-7723-4f99-bbed-3c70046e7c48	2014-03-23 19:25:24.177	30	\N	added 54 characters in body	The article is correct in the way it uses 4:1 and 5:1. Under their assumptions (actual value given their example is more like 4.2:1), you are "4 to 1" to make it while you are getting "5 to 1" on your money. I'd say that this is *precisely* because both are written / pronounced / thought of this way that it's convenient.\r\n\r\nIf you check the Wikipedia article on odds, you'll see that they're explaining it like in the article you quoted:\r\n\r\n[en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odds][1]\r\n\r\n> For example, a probability 0.20 is represented as "4 to 1 against"\r\n> (written as 4-1, 4:1, or 4/1), since there are five outcomes of which\r\n> four are unsuccessful.\r\n\r\nHowever the article you quoted is not correct or, at least, poorly worded. The article talks about what happens if you play 4:1 odds (doing an EV+ call by getting 5 to 1 on your call) and says that, mathematically, you'll hit your draw once if you run four deals like that.\r\n\r\nOdds of 4:1 corresponds to a probability *p = 0.20*.\r\n\r\nWell, if you play four deals like with *p = 0.20*, there's still a p = 0.4096 (40.96%) that you lose the deal all four times (0.8 exp 4 gives 0.4096).\r\n\r\nSo you "only" have *(1 - p)*, which gives 0.5904 (59.04%) chance you'll win *at least* one of the four deals.\r\n\r\nNow it is correct to say that you should always (at least) call when you have explicit odds in your favor: this is immensely EV+. *(let alone the implied odds and discussions about fold equity if you are the one pushing all in semi-bluffing your draws but I digress)*\r\n\r\nBut it is not correct to say that *"If you make this call four times, mathematics says that you will hit your draw once"*. That's not what probabilities says.\r\n\r\nProbabilities says that with p = 0.20 and four runs you have:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% chance to lose all four deals\r\n- 40.96% chance to win one the four deals\r\n- 15.36% chance to win two of the four deals\r\n-  2.56% chance to win three of the four deals\r\n-  0.16% chance to win all four deals\r\n\r\nThis gives, on average, 0.8 unit per deal if you're 5:1 on your call. If you play four times your EV is 3.2 units.\r\n\r\nThe article is definitely not correct saying that math says you'll hit your draw once: that would be an EV of 2 units (3 times losing 1, one time winning 5), which is not correct.\r\n\r\nTo verify this, I just wrote a "game" (mimicking the 4:1 odds of winning and the 5:1 payoff): it is either paying you 5 "units" when the random number is between [0..19] inclusive or removing you 1 unit when the random number is between [20.99] inclusive.\r\n\r\nI then ran 20 series of 4 "deals". Here are the results of a sample run:\r\n\r\n    [2 2 -4 2 -4 2 2 -4 -4 2 8 8 -4 2 -4 2 2 8 -4 -4]\r\n\r\nAs you can see, quite often you'll still lose the four deals: getting -4 because you lost four times 1 unit.\r\n\r\nI did verify the frequencies I wrote above over 10 millions runs and got this:\r\n\r\n    {-4  0.4096233,   2  0.4098634,   8  0.1533343,   14  0.0255836,   20  0.0015954}\r\n\r\nSo if you run four deals:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% of the time you'll lose four units (lose four times)\r\n- 40.96% of the time you'll win 2 units (lose 1 three times, win 5 once)\r\n- 15.36% of the time you'll win 8 units (lose 2 two times, win 5 twice)\r\n-  2.56% of the time you'll win 14 units (lose 1 once, win 5 three times)\r\n-  0.16% of the time you'll win 20 units (win four times)\r\n\r\nFor what it's worth, here's the full (Clojure) code used to verify these numbers: \r\n\r\n    (frequencies \r\n     (take 1000000\r\n           (repeatedly\r\n            #(apply +\r\n                    (for [x (range 4)]\r\n                      (if (< (rand-int 100) 20) 5 -1))))))\r\n\r\nAnd here's one way to verify that the EV is indeed EV+, winning you 0.8 "unit" per deal:\r\n\r\n    (/ (apply + (take 1000000\r\n           (repeatedly\r\n            #(apply +\r\n                    (for [x (range 4)]\r\n                      (if (< (rand-int 100) 20) 5 -1)))))) 1000000.0)\r\n\r\nWhich gives: 0.806582 unit per deal (close enough to the theoretical 0.8)\r\n\r\nNow of course where I agree with the article is that it is EV+ to call getting 5:1 on your call when you're 4:1 to hit the winning hand.\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odds
5599	5	2823	a77bf455-903e-4072-bc1a-d68f9ffcd3a2	2014-03-23 19:32:31.887	30	\N	added 54 characters in body	The article is correct in the way it uses 4:1 and 5:1. Under their assumptions (actual value given their example is more like 4.2:1), you are "4 to 1" to make it while you are getting "5 to 1" on your money. I'd say that this is *precisely* because both are written / pronounced / thought of this way that it's convenient.\r\n\r\nIf you check the Wikipedia article on odds, you'll see that they're explaining it like in the article you quoted:\r\n\r\n[en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odds][1]\r\n\r\n> For example, a probability 0.20 is represented as "4 to 1 against"\r\n> (written as 4-1, 4:1, or 4/1), since there are five outcomes of which\r\n> four are unsuccessful.\r\n\r\nHowever the article you quoted is not correct or, at least, poorly worded. The article talks about what happens if you play 4:1 odds (doing an EV+ call by getting 5 to 1 on your call) and says that, mathematically, you'll hit your draw once if you run four deals like that.\r\n\r\nOdds of 4:1 corresponds to a probability *p = 0.20*.\r\n\r\nWell, if you play four deals like with *p = 0.20*, there's still a p = 0.4096 (40.96%) that you lose the deal all four times (0.8 exp 4 gives 0.4096).\r\n\r\nSo you "only" have *(1 - p)*, which gives 0.5904 (59.04%) chance you'll win *at least* one of the four deals.\r\n\r\nNow it is correct to say that you should always (at least) call when you have explicit odds in your favor: this is immensely EV+. *(let alone the implied odds and discussions about fold equity if you are the one pushing all in semi-bluffing your draws but I digress)*\r\n\r\nBut it is not correct to say that *"If you make this call four times, mathematics says that you will hit your draw once"*. That's not what probabilities says.\r\n\r\nProbabilities says that with p = 0.20 and four runs you have:\r\n\r\n- 40.96% chance to lose all four deals\r\n- 40.96% chance to win one the four deals\r\n- 15.36% chance to win two of the four deals\r\n-  2.56% chance to win three of the four deals\r\n-  0.16% chance to win all four deals\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odds
5600	5	2824	989f3931-51a3-47a2-8a8a-f6acccffa2fd	2014-03-23 23:24:10.873	241	\N	added 672 characters in body	Since 4:1 are the correct odds for that scenario, it seems that the sentence `"If you make this call four times, mathematics says that you will hit your draw once."` was a mistake, rather than an error. Their intention was probably to say that for every four times you lose you'll win one, or for every five times you play you'll win one. In any case, your understanding of the concept "odds" is correct.\r\n\r\nTo be precise, the probability of hitting an out is 9 / (52 - 2 - 3) = 0.19 that expressed as odds is 4.22:1.\r\n\r\nAlso worth mentioning this:\r\n\r\n> Your opponent bets $1. There is now $5 in the pot ($4 + $1), and it is\r\n> $1 to call. The pot odds are therefore 5:1.\r\n\r\nis a simplification and it's not an entirely valid number to compare to your cards odds. To calculate the actual pot odds you'd have to predict if the players after you will call or even reraise. That is, you need to predict the odds of the total amount you'll end up contributing to the pot in the current street to the size of the pot at the end of the street plus the possibility that you collect the pot if you reraise and the rest of the players fold (if it's you who folds then there's nothing to calculate).
