5831	2	2912	d16cc279-2332-4fe3-80ee-33a2025c86e6	2014-04-26 15:59:45.133	\N	user1165	\N	i hope this question is not too vague, i think it's rather a common problem in micros where tiny money can be made or lost in relatively little time due to loose play or absurd aggression.\r\n<br>I am playing in *6-max cash games $0.01 - $0.02 microstakes* and always multitabling with **2 tables** at the same moment.\r\n<br>Frequently, when i sit down on tables i'm always thinking about the duration of game or my goal in it. I say to myself **gain this much and leave** or **play this much and leave**.\r\n<br>I once heard the best approach is to **stay while you're winning, leave when you're losing** and i am quite sure this is the correct approach.\r\n<br>Still, i find myself quite frequently in a position where i won, say, **100bb** or so and just want to leave, preserving what i gained but playing only about **~100** hands on less than *1 hour* session.\r\n>_1) Are NL2 micros for building your bankroll to move on higher stakes rather for money winning purpose ?_\r\n\r\nI always have that fear of losing what i won. Any help? It's better, say, playing 400,500 hands a day just to *get-used* to these stakes rather than trying to make real money with it?\r\n>_2) What do you consider enough for a starter trying to practice his book theory ? playing tons of hands per-day or winning that much or get out when you bored / tired / distracted etc. ?_\r\n\r\nI know this is vague but what did you do when you started about this type of *management*? I find it rather difficult to open new tables when i already won some cash.\r\ntx
