7502	5	4174	afe5b340-f347-4778-aa63-7ee781f1e48d	2014-08-31 07:07:40.563	309	\N	made the cards look like... cards :)	On the flop in hold-em, it is not possible for one player to have a straight flush (or even just a straight or just a flush) and another to have a full house.  A full house requires at least two cards on the board to have the same rank. A straight (or flush, or straight flush) on the flop requires that no cards on the flop be of the same rank.  These are mutually exclusive, so it's not possible.\r\n\r\nOn a full five-card board, though, it's entirely possible.  Say two players had these hole cards:\r\n\r\n1. A&hearts; A&diamond;\r\n2. J&spades; T&spades;\r\n\r\nand the board runs out like so: A&spades; K&spades; Q&spades; Q&hearts; 5&hearts;\r\n\r\nThen the players' final hands are:\r\n\r\n1. A&hearts; A&diamond; A&spades; Q&spades; Q&hearts; -- a Full House, Aces full of Queens.\r\n2. A&spades; K&spades; Q&spades; J&spades; T&spades; -- a Royal Flush.\r\n\r\n\r\nSo, no it's not possible on the flop, but it's entirely possible by the turn and river.
