7562	5	2875	0622af2a-3b4c-422c-a2d7-25d1d50bbce6	2014-09-13 20:21:39.903	1416	\N	Moving my comments to the reply, now that I have enough reputation to actually edit my own posts...	Games that are closer to "real" poker would not be *simple* enough to be solved. Recent research seems to be mostly focused on HU LHE and programming AI bots to play Game Theoretic Optimally. Most of this work seems to come out of the University of Alberta's Computer Poker Research Group. [You can find their publications here][1]. \r\n\r\nOne game that is closer to real poker, but not so complex as to be unsolvable is Rhode Island Hold'em. [You can read more about it here][2]. Even in this game, there are 3.1 billion nodes.\r\n\r\nHere are some related articles that may be of interest: \r\n\r\n - [Rhode Island Hold'em][3]\r\n - [Abstraction of Imperfect Information Games][4]\r\n\r\nBy the way, there's nothing to stop you from creating your own simplified poker games that are closer to "real" poker, and solving them yourself. Obvious changes you can do are removing streets, removing cards, capping the raises at a lower number of bets. It should be an enlightening exercise.\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://poker.cs.ualberta.ca/publications.html\r\n  [2]: http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~gilpin/gsi.html\r\n  [3]: http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sandholm/RIHoldEmWithScreenShots.aaai05reviewed.pdf\r\n  [4]: http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~gilpin/papers/extensive.JACM.pdf
