8035	2	4388	67fa391e-33b6-4ce6-8ddf-a07684a4c6b4	2014-11-26 20:31:30.083	1762	\N	\N	Nothing, besides perhaps criminal investigation. Poker is not a sport with a sanctioning body like the NBA, NFL etc, that has sanctioning powers in this respect. \r\n\r\nCasino promoters, (Use this term as a catch all for the operators and organizers) have regular powers to disqualify players, but unless there was some kind of incident, like a fist fight at the final table for example, I don't think they would disqualify someone for being stoned or high. I don't think they would have the legal power to disqualify someone after the fact for the use of PED's, nor do the have a right to verify it the use of PED's with say a drug test.\r\n\r\nPoker tournaments are open venue, meaning anyone can register. The only thing that qualifies one as a player is their ability to make the buy-in. I think considering the lifestyle of many poker pros, that a subjection to a clause concerning PED's would not go over to well.  
