10722	5	6035	0373591f-b4c0-4719-98d7-f314d4079df9	2015-07-01 13:37:17.437	3316	\N	deleted 1110 characters in body	On the flop, the best you can do is the chance that all three cards will come up the same suit.   So you have 2 of them, that leaves 11 more.  There are 50 unkown cards in the deck.  so 11/50 (or 22%) to hit the first card.   it then goes to 10/49 (or 20.4%) for the second and 9/48 (or 19.5%) for the third.\r\n\r\nTo calculate all three of those events happening "simultaneously" the formula would be (i think) ... \r\n\r\ntotalCardsRemaining : totalCardsRemaing ^ 3 - totalCardsRemaining\r\n\r\nUsing the average of 49 ...\r\n\r\n49 : 49 ^ 3 - 49 = 49 : 117,600 = 1 : 2400 hands maybe...\r\n\r\nI think that would be probability and not odds though..\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n
