10759	2	6050	a131624b-21b2-409b-806e-cdbf8b918730	2015-07-05 18:13:30.403	3456	\N	\N	just trying to gather some more information on bankroll strategy. I have recently starting taking poker seriously as a second income opportunity. \r\n\r\nI live in country Australia where we have terrible internet services so I can't play online. I travel 2 hours by car (on a good day) to the nearest casino. The lowest stakes are $2/$3 with a min buy-in of $100 and max $300. Rules of a "normal" bankroll has a minimum of 20 buy-ins. I buy-in for $200 which means I should have a min $4000. I currently have a bankroll of $1100.\r\n\r\nCurrent rules (with wifes influence lol)\r\n-I must only ever enter $200 and once ive lost that i go home.\r\n-I have a stop loss of $200 after making $50. So if my stack at its highest for the day was $550 and I am down to $450 (-$100) I then must pull out at a stack of $350 (-$200).\r\n-Bankroll accumilation: I started with $200, I have a ceiling of $800 bankroll. Any profits after I have $800 is split. 20% goes to building my bankroll while the other 80% goes toward personal spending and bills. \r\n-All expences must come out of bankroll such as food, drink, petrol and toll fees.\r\n\r\nIs there any other information or advice you guys can give me that would help me. is there anything i should add or takeaway. As stated, i know i should have a bankroll of about $4000 and i need to stick to the 80/20 split to keep the wife happy. Also note that it does seem like overkill to drive 2 hours 160km (99.4miles) one way to play poker and even more overkill to no that I may only play 5 minutes of poker to lose my $200 and then leave but I truely believe that if I am this dedicated and have strong discipline now, it will surely help me down the track.\r\n\r\nSorry for the novel I have just written, its my first question and thought I really need to explain the situation to get some great feedback. I appreciate all future comments. 
