11607	2	6366	0c90221d-4618-4289-a323-01db89db6110	2015-10-25 03:25:01.97	3758	\N	\N	According to the [Fundamental Theorem of Poker](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fundamental_theorem_of_poker), our expectation is increased every time the opponents make a wrong decision.\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, [Morton's Theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morton's_theorem) states that a player's expectation may be maximized by an opponent making a correct decision.\r\n\r\nPersonally, it appears to me that Morton's theorem is absurd. My reasoning would be that since poker is a zero sum game, the correct decision of an opponent by definition, would mean the one that minimizes the opponents expectations.\r\n\r\nIt appears that both theorems contradict each other. Which one of them is correct?
