11692	2	6396	c0833bbe-9599-4d27-88da-70250946f6f5	2015-11-03 14:47:32.593	3511	\N	\N	The chance that any one person has AA when you have no aces (and given no other information) is 6 / (50 * 49 / 2) or ~ .5% (note: you know your two cards, so it's not 52 * 51).  This means that there's a 99.5% chance that they don't.  When doing probabilities the probability that any one of n things with probability p_n is going to happen is 1 minus the the product of 1 minus the p_n's: i.e. the chance that any one of them happens is 1 minus the chance of all of them *not* happening, the correct answer is 1 - (1 - .005)^(number of seats not counting you).  Rounding to .1%:\r\n\r\n    1: .5% (~1 in 200 heads up)\r\n    2: 1%\r\n    3: 1.5%\r\n    4: 2%  (1 in 50)\r\n    5: 2.5% (1 in 40 six-handed)\r\n    6: 3%\r\n    7: 3.5%\r\n    8: 4% (1 in 25 full 9-player table)
