12275	2	6631	7643069f-1be0-4b0c-8db3-290f1c66f675	2016-01-15 17:28:07.477	3876	\N	\N	When you are short stacked you unfortunately don't have the chips to force a bad decision.  \r\n\r\nAccept the opponent is not going to fold.  1/4 pot bet with nothing behind is not going to get them off a flush draw at the flop.  With nothing behind they get two cards without the risk of facing another bet.  You would need a pot size bet to get them off a flush draw.  \r\n\r\nAt this point you need to play your odds and at 2:1 favorite you want your money in the pot.  \r\n\r\nAs a short stack you don't have the luxury of waiting for premium hands.  Even AA against 6 random hands is a coin flip.  You don't have the chips to isolate so you are basically playing the table.  Depending on your starting hand you may have been better off getting it in pre flop.\r\n
12276	5	6631	fd0889d0-7719-4a11-b08c-18f5071a8fdd	2016-01-15 17:39:12.407	3876	\N	added 126 characters in body	When you are short stacked you unfortunately don't have the chips to force a bad decision.  \r\n\r\nAccept the opponent is not going to fold.  1/4 pot bet with nothing behind is not going to get them off a flush draw at the flop.  With nothing behind they get two cards without the risk of facing another bet.  You would need a pot size bet to get them off a flush draw.  \r\n\r\nAt this point you need to play your odds and at 2:1 favorite you want your money in the pot.  \r\n\r\nAs a short stack you don't have the luxury of waiting for premium hands.  Even AA against 6 random hands is a coin flip.  You don't have the chips to isolate so you are basically playing the table.  \r\n\r\nDepending on your starting hand you may have been better off getting it in pre flop.  Even if you were the BB and raised to 2BB that is 5 BB in the pot.  How did you end up with a stack size of 1/4 the pot?\r\n
12277	2	6632	38ff6b0a-ab6e-4bee-8d66-b654c511a5da	2016-01-15 17:46:56.11	3654	\N	\N	We need a bit more information.  Starting stacks, bets pre-flop etc.  From what it sounds like so far you should have pushed all in pre-flop or after the flop.\r\n\r\nOne thing I disagree with however is when you said that in the long run you would lose money to a flush draw.  If you are positive you have him beat and the only thing that will save him is if he hits a card making his flush you will win more times than you will lose.  He has at most 9 cards that will hit his draw and only one chance to get it, that is about 18%.  So 82 % of the time you are winning.\r\n\r\nI would make that push/shove all day long if you're positive they are on a flush draw.
