12502	5	6699	d03a8a64-1e98-4ab3-87dd-490d0733bb39	2016-02-06 17:39:33.317	3876	\N	added 44 characters in body	I have seen it on TV a few times  \r\n\r\n\r\nBased on hand [independent][1] it is 0.000256 (quad) * 0.0017 (boat) = 0.00000044     \r\n\r\nBut since they share a pair on the board less than that   \r\nI think the real calc is 0.0017 * 0.00101 = 0.00000172\r\n\r\nFrom the boat there are 45 cards out and need to match the pair on the board   \r\nSo the odds of getting *quadded* are:     \r\n[(2/2) / (45/2)][2] = 0.00101    \r\nWhich also = 2/45 * 1/44\r\n\r\nHighest or lower boat is immaterial.  Quads beat a full house. \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability\r\n  [2]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination\r\n  [3]: http://
12503	2	6706	ea34710d-5420-4e4a-a5ee-0eb0a3427b02	2016-02-07 06:29:15.633	1084	\N	\N	Frisbee's answer is correct. But it only answers for eight players (although the other numbers are hidden in that table). Since the OP asked for 8-10, I will add the answers for nine and ten players.\r\n\r\nThe odds of an ace not occurring in the next `x` cards dealt (after you're dealt two aces) is `( 48!/(48-x)! )/( 50!/(50-x)! )`. That's the probability that no one else has an ace, so the probability that someone else has an ace is [the complement][1] of that. We can easily check the probabilities [using this lookup table][2].\r\n\r\nThe number of cards left to be dealt is `2*(p - 1)` where `p` is the number of players, so for 8, 9, and 10 players, the number of cards left is 14, 16, and 18 respectively.\r\n\r\nThe lookup table gives us the probabilities `0.458714`, `0.542041` and `0.595102` respectively. For completeness, the probability of at least one opponent having at least one ace, when the table has `p` players is:\r\n\r\n     p | P( Another player has an ace | We have pocket aces)\r\n    -------------------------------------------------------\r\n     2 | 7.91837%\r\n     3 | 15.5102%\r\n     4 | 22.7755%\r\n     5 | 29.7143%\r\n     6 | 36.3265%\r\n     7 | 42.6122%\r\n     8 | 48.5712%\r\n     9 | 54.2041%\r\n    10 | 59.5102%\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complementary_event\r\n  [2]: http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=N%5B1-%20(%2048!%2F(48-x)!%20)%2F(%2050!%2F(50%20-%20x)!%20)%5D,%20where%20x%20in%20(2,18)
12520	5	6552	7b792c49-13e2-47a4-813b-6809b833918b	2016-02-09 01:38:24.513	3876	\N	added 48 characters in body	Well you can add as a draw is elusive  \r\n\r\nThis is a flush draw using [combination][1]  \r\nYou have 50 cards left and   \r\nfrom a set of 3 you want two that are the same suite  \r\nThe suite you want has 11 cards   \r\n39 is the count of other cards = 50-11  \r\n(11/2) is not a fraction - it is [binomial coefficient][2]  \r\n<b>4 flush (11/2)(39/1)/(50/3) = 0.1094 = 8.1 : 1</B>  \r\n\r\nIf you add in flopping the whole flush (11/3) then you get the 7.5 : 1 you are more used to seeing    \r\n<b>4 or five flush ((11/2)(39/1) + (11/3) - 4) / (50/3)</b>     \r\nthe - 4 is straight flush  \r\nyyy xx   \r\nyy xx y  \r\ny xx yy  \r\nxx yyy   \r\n\r\nCalculating a straight draw is much more difficult  \r\n    \r\nAs for 4 draw you would just add them as a straight flush can still make a straight and not a flush and visa versa   \r\nNow as for a the 5 card hand you would need to discount out the straight-flush from either as it is a separate hand   \r\n\r\nIt gets complex enough that you just need to run simulations in a package like R to do your shuffles for you.  check this [link][3] on straight from 7 cards.  \r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination\r\n  [2]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient\r\n  [3]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability
12504	5	6706	2c43ac1a-e8f0-4f91-8883-646145a636c5	2016-02-07 06:36:38.463	1084	\N	added 1 character in body	Frisbee's answer is correct. But it only answers for eight players (although the other numbers are hidden in that table). Since the OP asked for 8-10, I will add the answers for nine and ten players.\r\n\r\nThe odds of an ace not occurring in the next `x` cards dealt (after you're dealt two aces) is `( 48!/(48-x)! )/( 50!/(50-x)! )`. That's the probability that no one else has an ace, so the probability that someone else has an ace is [the complement][1] of that. We can easily check the probabilities [using this lookup table][2].\r\n\r\nThe number of cards left to be dealt is `2*(p - 1)` where `p` is the number of players, so for 8, 9, and 10 players, the number of cards left is 14, 16, and 18 respectively.\r\n\r\nThe lookup table gives us the probabilities `0.458714`, `0.542041` and `0.595102` respectively. For completeness, the probability of at least one opponent having at least one ace, when the table has `p` players is:\r\n\r\n     p | P( Another player has an ace | We have pocket aces)\r\n    -------------------------------------------------------\r\n     2 |  7.91837%\r\n     3 | 15.5102%\r\n     4 | 22.7755%\r\n     5 | 29.7143%\r\n     6 | 36.3265%\r\n     7 | 42.6122%\r\n     8 | 48.5712%\r\n     9 | 54.2041%\r\n    10 | 59.5102%\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complementary_event\r\n  [2]: http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=N%5B1-%20(%2048!%2F(48-x)!%20)%2F(%2050!%2F(50%20-%20x)!%20)%5D,%20where%20x%20in%20(2,18)
