p and the probably would have bet\r\n   the flop\r\n\r\n - 58 villain would not have even called preflop\r\n\r\n - A8, A5 villain would only call from mid if suited      \r\nAnd villain would have probably bet the flop   \r\nnot a likely holding  \r\n   \r\n - 55, 88 villain totally would have played that way    \r\nyou seriously need to consider that you are dominated here      \r\nat a 4 outer you are not getting even close to pot odds   \r\ncall is probably wrong - if villain has you beat he/she is going to bet out on the river     \r\nif villain is bluffing then should bluff again on the river   \r\nraise or fold and you seriously need to consider fold  \r\n\r\n - on top of all that villain could be on a flush draw now but that would be an odd bet for a flush draw  \r\n \r\n - jack was a scare card for the villain as you could be on AJ, KQ,\r\n   diamonds, or a pair > 5 so villain is happy with a fold here to get you off a draw    \r\n   no way villain is coming off their hand as they are only behind AA, 88 or\r\n   JJ and if you had JJ you would bet flop   \r\nif villain was just value betting the set it would have been more like 1/3 - 1/2 the pot and then 1/3 - 1/2 the pot on the river - they wanted you gone or all in  \r\n\r\n    \r\n   \r\n 
12518	5	6552	6a5efd02-4f10-4e6c-abba-9dadecc7ce89	2016-02-09 00:38:22.29	3876	\N	added 963 characters in body	Well you can add as a draw is elusive  \r\n\r\nThis is a flush draw using [combination][1]\r\n\r\nYou have 50 cards left and   \r\nfrom a set of 3 you want two that are the same suite  \r\nThe suite you want has 11 cards   \r\n39 is the count of other cards = 50-11\r\n(11/2) is not a fraction - it is [binomial coefficient][2]\r\n(11/2)(39/1)/(50/3) = 0.1094 = 8.1 : 1  \r\n\r\nIf you add in flopping the whole flush (11/3) then you get the 7.5 : 1 you are more used to seeing    \r\n((11/2)(39/1) + (11/3) - 3) / (50/3)   \r\nthe - 3 is straight flush  \r\n\r\nCalculating a straight draw is much more difficult  \r\n    \r\nAs for 4 draw you would just add them as a straight flush can still make a straight and not a flush and visa versa   \r\nNow as for a the 5 card hand you would need to discount out the straight-flush from either as it is a separate hand   \r\n\r\nIt gets complex enough that you just need to run simulations in a package like R to do your shuffles for you.\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination\r\n  [2]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient
12519	5	6552	ce35207e-4692-4688-a93c-ff6629a72714	2016-02-09 00:45:16.887	3876	\N	added 40 characters in body	Well you can add as a draw is elusive  \r\n\r\nThis is a flush draw using [combination][1]\r\nYou have 50 cards left and   \r\nfrom a set of 3 you want two that are the same suite  \r\nThe suite you want has 11 cards   \r\n39 is the count of other cards = 50-11\r\n(11/2) is not a fraction - it is [binomial coefficient][2]  \r\n<b>4 flush (11/2)(39/1)/(50/3) = 0.1094 = 8.1 : 1</B>  \r\n\r\nIf you add in flopping the whole flush (11/3) then you get the 7.5 : 1 you are more used to seeing    \r\n<b>4 or five flush ((11/2)(39/1) + (11/3) - 3) / (50/3)</b>     \r\nthe - 3 is straight flush  \r\n\r\nCalculating a straight draw is much more difficult  \r\n    \r\nAs for 4 draw you would just add them as a straight flush can still make a straight and not a flush and visa versa   \r\nNow as for a the 5 card hand you would need to discount out the straight-flush from either as it is a separate hand   \r\n\r\nIt gets complex enough that you just need to run simulations in a package like R to do your shuffles for you.  check this [link][3] on straight from 7 cards.  \r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination\r\n  [2]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient\r\n  [3]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability
12521	5	6552	15d8c50e-61ca-4776-891d-553428272b44	2016-02-09 01:43:55.127	3876	\N	added 7 characters in body	Well you can add as a draw is elusive  \r\n\r\nThis is a flush draw using [combination][1]  \r\nYou have 50 cards left and   \r\nfrom a set of 3 you want two that are the same suite  \r\nThe suite you want has 11 cards   \r\n39 is the count of other cards = 50-11  \r\n(11/2) is not a fraction - it is [binomial coefficient][2]  \r\n<b>4 flush (11/2)(39/1)/(50/3) = 0.1094 = 8.1 : 1</B>  \r\n\r\nIf you add in flopping the whole flush (11/3) then you get the <b>7.5 : 1</b> you are more used to seeing    \r\n<b>4 or five flush ((11/2)(39/1) + (11/3) - 4) / (50/3)</b>     \r\nthe - 4 is straight flush  \r\nyyy xx   \r\nyy xx y  \r\ny xx yy  \r\nxx yyy   \r\n\r\nCalculating a straight draw is much more difficult  \r\n    \r\nAs for 4 draw you would just add them as a straight flush can still make a straight and not a flush and visa versa   \r\nNow as for a the 5 card hand you would need to discount out the straight-flush from either as it is a separate hand   \r\n\r\nIt gets complex enough that you just need to run simulations in a package like R to do your shuffles for you.  check this [link][3] on straight from 7 cards.  \r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination\r\n  [2]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient\r\n  [3]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability
