12623	2	6747	5d43a152-26c6-4e96-8016-fd63a284d3b9	2016-02-19 03:32:34.793	3876	\N	\N	Naturally the odd of trips, full house, and four of a kind goes down as you are not holding a pair.  \r\n\r\nI ran the number and here are the results:  \r\n\r\n - trips, full house, four of a kind    \r\n1.44%  \r\n   0.63 x compared to a random hand   \r\nodds go down by 1/3\r\n\r\n - flush   \r\n   0.84%   \r\n4.28 x compared to a random hand\r\n\r\n - straight   \r\n   1.3%     \r\n3.34 x compared to a random hand\r\n\r\n - two pair of better   \r\n   <b>11.7% =  9.6 : 1</b>  \r\n   1.53 x compared to a random hand \r\n\r\nIf the board pairs and you don't have the top then be careful as few ways you are beat.  But otherwise if you hit 2 pair or better you are very likely ahead and will hold on to the river.   \r\n\r\nSo if I think I can get 10:1 implied odds on the call then do it.  \r\n\r\nAdd on top of that about a 12% chance of a flush draw or 4 outer straight draw.  \r\n\r\nA 1 gapper does not change the number much (but a 2 gapper does).\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n
12624	2	6748	63c21e0f-9334-4ef3-a46e-13666595a7c5	2016-02-19 03:50:18.917	3816	\N	\N	Based on figures from http://www.flopturnriver.com/poker-strategy/pyroxenes-common-flop-odds-19147, the odds of flopping 2 pair or better, not including draws, is just over 5.6%.\r\n\r\nIncluding flopping a straight or flush draw, the odds are just a hair over 27%.\r\n\r\nIf your facing a raise and re-raise, you should probably fold, as you're not getting the right odds.\r\n\r\nGenerally you want to play suited connectors in late position or the blinds, especially when there are several callers, so that you're getting good odds.
12625	5	6747	c04719e3-4a56-45ca-a950-9f51348d241d	2016-02-19 03:50:47.9	3876	\N	added 200 characters in body	Naturally the odd of trips, full house, and four of a kind goes down as you are not holding a pair.  \r\n\r\nI ran the number and here are the results:  \r\n\r\n - trips, full house, four of a kind    \r\n1.44%  \r\n   0.63 x compared to a random hand   \r\nodds go down by 1/3\r\n\r\n - flush   \r\n   0.84%   \r\n4.28 x compared to a random hand\r\n\r\n - straight   \r\n   1.3%     \r\n3.34 x compared to a random hand\r\n\r\n - two pair of better   \r\n   <b>11.7% =  9.6 : 1</b>  \r\n   1.53 x compared to a random hand \r\n\r\nIf the board pairs and you don't have the top then be careful as few ways you are beat.  But otherwise if you hit 2 pair or better you are very likely ahead.  Straight or flush clearly the risk is a 4 straight or flush on the turn or river so don't slow play.  \r\n\r\nIf you think you can get 10:1 implied odds on the call then do it.  \r\n\r\nAdd on top of that about a 12% chance of a flush draw or 4 outer straight draw.   \r\n\r\nA 1 gapper does not change the number much (but a 2 gapper does).  \r\n\r\nMid to late position against deep stack(s) if can get in for 4:1 immediate pot odds then a very payable hand down to 53s.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n
12637	5	6751	cdb63993-5677-4fc9-bdbb-09e471481e09	2016-02-20 04:00:07.717	3876	\N	added 2 characters in body	 - player 1   \r\nK:hearts:Q:diamonds:  \r\n - player 2   \r\n4:hearts:3:hearts: \r\n - board    \r\nQ:hearts:6:hearts:5:spades:3:diamonds:\r\n\r\nplayer 1 is ahead    \r\nplayer 2 must improve but player 1 can improve also   \r\n\r\na flush or straight for player 2 wins out right   \r\n8 hearts + 6 straight = 14 outs  \r\n52 - 8 = 44 cards out  \r\nwin flush or straight = 14 / (44-14) = 14 / 30 = 0.4667\r\n  \r\nbut player 2 could draw a 4 for two pair - 3 outs     \r\nbut a K or Q for player 1 would kill it - 5 outs   \r\n3 / (44-3) * (1 - 5/(43-5)) = 0.0635\r\n\r\nplayer 2 could draw a 3 for trips - 2 outs   \r\nbut a Q for player 1 would kill it - 2 outs     \r\n2 / (44-2) * (1 - 2/(43-2) = 0.0453  \r\n\r\n**total player 2 = 0.5755**\r\n\r\n**total player 1** = 1 - player 2 = **0.4245**\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n 
