13207	2	6938	323a577b-8e7a-43d5-9486-02b60750700a	2016-05-05 17:31:58.06	\N	user1934	\N	The logic is that you can't win more against any other player than you bet, and when you fold you forfeit all bets you have made into the pot.  For any given pot the best hand that has an interest in that pot (has money in it) wins that pot.  Your amounts of 120 and 200 are correct. To break it down:\r\n\r\nPlayer A all in for 40.\r\n\r\nPlayer B raises to 80.  Main pot 40(A)+40(B), side pot has 40(B).\r\n\r\nPlayer C raises to 200. Main pot 40(A)+40(B)+40(C), side pot has 40(B)+160(C).\r\n\r\nPlayer B folds, forfeiting side pot of 200 to B. (A does not have an interest in this side pot.)\r\n\r\nPlayer A wins at showdown, and gets main pot of 120.\r\n
13208	5	6938	51c91cf7-16f1-4093-8f00-bf6b153fcc75	2016-05-05 19:28:41.543	\N	user1934	clarified folding versus not folding	The logic is that you can't win more against any other player than you bet, and when you fold you forfeit all bets you have made into the pot.  For any given pot the best (or last, if everyone else has folded) hand that has an interest in that pot (has money in it and hasn't folded) wins that pot.  Your amounts of 120 and 200 are correct. To break it down:\r\n\r\nPlayer A all in for 40.\r\n\r\nPlayer B raises to 80.  Main pot 40(A)+40(B), side pot has 40(B).\r\n\r\nPlayer C raises to 200. Main pot 40(A)+40(B)+40(C), side pot has 40(B)+160(C).\r\n\r\nPlayer B folds, forfeiting side pot of 200 to B. (A does not have an interest in this side pot.)\r\n\r\nPlayer A wins at showdown, and gets main pot of 120.\r\n
