13225	2	6944	7993d1e0-b610-4c28-935b-8bf0bf92158c	2016-05-10 00:00:22.647	4285	\N	\N	Many sources (including [here][1], [here][2], [here][3], and [here][4]) explicitly list Royal Flush as the highest poker hand. This seems unnecessary - it's the highest hand anyway as the highest Straight Flush. When designing a ruleset, unnecessary is harmful (e.g. it gives new players more to remember). So is there a good historical or game-mechanics reason why we treat the Royal Flush as a special case?\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://www.facebook.com/notes/vnh-poker/list-of-poker-hands/235365729873152/\r\n  [2]: http://www.texasholdem-poker.com/handrank\r\n  [3]: http://www.fulltilt.com/poker/hand-rankings\r\n  [4]: http://www.cardplayer.com/rules-of-poker/hand-rankings
