14856	2	8162	696f0ca2-a8aa-483f-8c24-1b0b31283906	2016-08-08 16:34:15.617	3876	\N	\N	I don't think I would look at is like that.  \r\n\r\nLet's say you are 1/3 to hit the flush.  \r\n\r\nThe chance of not hitting is 2/3.  \r\n\r\nNot hitting twice in a row 44.44%\r\n\r\n    44.44% 2\r\n    29.63% 3\r\n    19.75% 4\r\n    13.17% 5\r\n    8.78% 6\r\n    5.85% 7\r\n    3.90% 8\r\n    2.60% 9\r\n    1.73% 10\r\n    1.16% 11\r\n    0.77% 12\r\n    0.51% 13\r\n    0.34% 14\r\n    0.23% 15\r\n    0.15% 16\r\n    0.10% 17\r\n    0.07% 18\r\n    0.05% 19\r\n    0.03% 20\r\n\r\nThere is 1.73% chance you don't hit 10 times in a row.  That is just plain not lucky but you should still play the numbers.  There is a lot of variance in poker and that is why you need a bankroll to absorb it.  \r\n\r\nYou just plain need to play more hands. Go with lower stakes games where you can afford some losses as you learn the game. \r\n\r\nIf you are playing a home game you can run the board multiple time to reduce variance.\r\n\r\nBy saying 1/3 indicates a hole in your game to me.  You are only 1/3 if you are getting two more cards. You are only getting two more cards for that price if you are all in.  I seriously doubt you had 10 all ins on the flop and 9 re-buys.\r\n\r\nOn the flop you have 9 outs and 47 cards to come. Your immediate hand odds are 38 / 9 = 4.22 : 1.  Unless you are all in or all you opponents are all in then you need pot odds of 4.22 : 1.  Yes you might get more money in the pot if you hit for better implied odds. But if you don't hit you are going to be facing another bet on the river.  Pot odds are not useless.  I suspect you are not playing proper odds and you will be absolutely abused by players playing proper odds.
