15290	5	8304	735bb9ff-5eee-49be-b267-c2542a3adb15	2016-10-13 19:45:00.347	4641	\N	added 153 characters in body	I'm currently watching the 1998 movie [Rounders][1] and I hope that i'm not spoilering for anyone. But even if, it's no a key scene. It's at the very beginning. \r\n\r\n - Matt Damon sits at a `4max` table as `UTG` with `A9s`. He raises 3x.\r\n - `UTG+1` folds, `SB`, played by John Malkovich, calls.\r\n - The Flop brings A9X and Matt Damon has `top two pair`.\r\n - Matt Damon overbets 4x pot size to bluff, that he wants to scare John Malkovich out of the hand\r\n - Turn brings 9 and the Full House for Matt Damon. He checks.\r\n - He assumes John Malkovich to have a Flush Draw.\r\n\r\n - John Malkovich bets, Matt Damon `2Bets` all in.\r\n - John Malkovich calls and shows `AAA99` and wins agains Matt Damon's\r\n   `999AA`\r\n\r\n**The question now is**, that I thought about that play and it would never happen to me, because I would have re-raised `preflop` with `AA`.\r\nI have further thought about that I could call as well, but the chance to bust against a lower pair that hits a set on the board is given and that's why I have always solid played good starting hands.\r\n\r\nIs calling a preflop bet with `AA` profitable longer term?\r\n\r\nThe situation proofs a call right, but in 1000 similar situations, would `AA` call be profitable? \r\n\r\nBecause I'm sure, in a real situation, Matt Damon (`A9s`) would have folded a raise by John Malkovich and the whole situation wouldn't have happened.\r\n\r\nPS: It looked for me like Cashgame. When John Malkovich won the hand, he left the table (with `UTG+1` and `BB` still in play). I feel like the fact if it is Cashgame or SNG does make an influence on the decision call/raise.\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0128442/
