15437	2	8355	2e2371c8-aed4-44bd-a874-ac05c766ddf8	2016-10-26 08:17:27.797	88	\N	\N	Kahnemans theory is slightly more nuanced. You are actually supporting his evidence with your example as he found that people are more risk averse *when they are winning* and conversely they are more risk seeking *when they are losing*. \n\nSo in your example, when a player is losing they may be likely to take actions that in retrospect are more risky/uncertain than the situation merits. That fits well within the theory. 
