15446	2	8360	4ba6ae44-30cf-4f38-84b4-7a580c25c90d	2016-10-26 16:54:05.02	3876	\N	\N	The easy case is bet the pot.  You need a fold 1/2 the time.  You risk 1 to win 2.\r\n\r\nr is the fraction of the post you bet    \r\nf is chance of a fold from you opponent   \r\nEV = 0 = -r + f*(1 + r)    \r\nr / (1 + r) = f  \r\n\r\n    r       f\r\n    0.50 0.33\r\n    0.67 0.40\r\n    1.00 0.50\r\n    1.50 0.60\r\n    2.00 0.67\r\n    3.00 0.75\r\n    4.00 0.80\r\n    5.00 0.83\r\n    6.00 0.86\r\n    7.00 0.88\r\n    8.00 0.89\r\n    9.00 0.90\r\n    10.00 0.91\r\n\r\nA big bet is big intimidation but you need to be pretty sure it going to fold them out.  At a 4x raise you need to fold them out 80% or the time.  And you need to be 80% sure they don't have a hand.\r\n\r\nGoing from 1 to 2x raise you have put twice as much at risk.  But your fold requirement goes up by .67 / .5 = 33%.  33% is not a good return when you need 50% to stay even.  \r\n\r\nThe down side is you need to play the nuts like a bluff so it is harder to stack them.  But you have the nuts at a 1x bluff there is a chance they will play back at.\r\n\r\nThe is the concept of small ball.  Putting twice as much at risk is typically not twice the reward.\r\n\r\n
