15507	5	8089	86d728c8-1e42-47fd-b3cd-70f9c3205fd3	2016-10-31 21:58:11.573	217	\N	added 1 character in body	A bet of 2.5x the pot IS a huge bet. Which is why you should pass with a straight when you see the third flush card. You can be beaten, not only by a flush, but by a 6-high straight (villain has 6-5).\r\n\r\nYou should have tested the waters by betting the turn, with your A-high "gutshot" straight draw. If villain has nothing, he'll fold. If he calls, he has a draw like you. If the last card fills a draw and he bets, he's made it, especially given the size of the bet.
15508	2	8382	18ecd876-fb74-4571-8449-ee1ae7cf9005	2016-10-31 21:59:36.83	4719	\N	\N	The probability of flopping either a straight draw or a flush draw is \r\n\r\n1-(P(Not flopping either one))\r\n\r\n1-(P(Not flopping either one)) = 1 - (1-0.109)*(1-0.104) =  1 - (0.891*0.896) = 1-0.798 = 0.202 = 20.2%.\r\n\r\nSo about 20%...
