15606	2	8420	f7d82397-c18b-430d-816f-9020fb7ecfeb	2016-11-10 10:30:42.903	4741	\N	\N	Paparazzi's answer is correct, but I would add a little bit more to that (especially that the examples were about full house over full house which is not that probable). \r\n\r\nWith one ace a lot depends on your kicker. Imagine that you have A8 and someone from early position raised pre flop. The flop comes AQ9. Are you happy with that flop? Not really - unless she/he is really loose opponent you should assume that he can have AQ, AQ, QQ, 99 and if he is loose maybe even A9. There are few other hands in his range you can beat but this puts you in a tricky situation.\r\n\r\nWith pocket aces this entirely different decision. If flop comes AQ9 and you have pocket aces you are really happy with that flop (unless it is suited maybe). Not only you have the best hand but also there is high chance that your opponent has decent hand and you will be able to extract some value from it.\r\n\r\nThere is also one more thing to consider - with pocket pair (especially aces) there is much less chance that villain puts you on a set than when the pair is already on the board. You get a pocket pair only one in 17 times and when you have a pocket pair odds of flopping a set are only 8.5 to 1. So it is much easier to get value from a set on a pocket pair because it is harder for your opponents to figure out that you actually have it.\r\n\r\nSo yes, it is of course better to have pocket rockets than one ace when you flop a set and it also applies to any pocket pair actually.
