15747	2	8464	c4bdc2fb-b5ff-41b7-87b3-fb5b2639910d	2016-12-02 16:57:27.757	4653	\N	\N	I disagree with the notion "A c bet is appropriate like 90% of the time. 10% don't do it (with both made and un-made hands) just so they cannot count on a c bet." made by Paparazzi, maybe in some game something like this is appropriate but this is 2016. The C-Betting secret is out, even if this is play money, C-Betting 90% is just plain bad. Using this poor logic makes your check back range (10%) very face up, either complete air balls or monster hands. Throw some middling pairs, top pair low kicker, and maybe a few of draws in your check back range. Doing this leaves your opponents guessing. Now that that is out of the way to the hand:\r\n\r\nPre-flop seems fine, if you move up to higher stakes you'll want to start making your opening size around 2.5x the BB, but here it's OK.\r\n\r\nI agree with your logic on why you want to cbet the flop, but don't like the sizing. I understand that you want to maximize the number of hands that fold, but using that sizing just shoots yourself in the foot(you'll see why later). You generally want to cbet anywhere from 1/4 to 1/2 the size of the pot.\r\n\r\nOn the turn, since you bet pot on both streets and bloated the pot, when you get check/raised you have to put in the rest of your stack with a very mediocre hand because you're getting a pretty good price. You would have left yourself the option to get away from this type of hand, if you used smaller sizes from the start.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n
15748	2	8465	f56a6872-fe8a-465d-a399-35e5072fd112	2016-12-02 17:11:17.687	3876	\N	\N	You have some good answers but bets are not limited to bluffs.  You need to size bets also for max value.  If you are on the nuts and don't suspect your opponent hit the flop then a pot size bet on the flop is wrong.\r\n\r\nYou need to size your bets to get max value, bluff, and not be predictable.  There is a lot to it.  On the flop if you bet 1/2 with mid pair or better, full pot with 2 pair or better, full pot with bluff, and check everything else you will be exploited by a mid range or better player.  \r\n\r\nBluff when you sense weakness and according to GTO you should bluff a fraction of the time.  For a pot size bet like 1/3 of the bets should be bluffs.  \r\n\r\nA good hedge is to semi bluff some draws where you have bluff equity and some hand equity.  I put that in my 1/3 bluffs and I can get away with very few stone cold bluffs unless I sense weakness.  I would also get killed by a pro.  You need to bluff within your overall ability.\r\n\r\nThe over bet is a very powerful bluff but if it does not work then it is a very costly bluff.   Too many amateurs push at like 10x the pot when 2x the pot would do the same thing.   Yes you see pros do it but they are pros.  Pros will do this to amateurs / fish.  First get off the table.  Second need to look them up with some hands good enough to be bluff catchers. \r\n\r\n
