15803	2	8482	acbcb0e0-f141-4dc0-8235-870faaba2523	2016-12-07 13:40:27.15	4771	\N	\N	Assuming that everyone goes to showdown every hand (which is obviously a massive and unrealistic assumption, but necessary to make the calculation manageable), the chances of at least one player in your 7-handed game makes quads on any given hand is: \r\n\r\n`0.168% * 7 = 1.176%`\r\n\r\nFor someone to get the same quads on another hand 1.is:\r\n\r\n`(0.168% / 13) * 7 = 0.0905%`\r\n\r\nSo for these two events to both occur would be:\r\n\r\n`1.176% * 0.0905% = 0.00106428%`\r\n\r\nFor someone to additionally then get any other 4OAK on the 3rd hand:\r\n\r\n`0.00106428% * (0.168% * 7) = 0.000000125159328%`\r\n\r\nor roughly **1 in 8 million**.\r\n\r\nTo be any more accurate, we need to model player behaviour and figure out chances that particular hands get to showdown etc, which makes the calculation non-trivial.\r\n
