16178	2	8600	ca4aacf6-8aa3-43ab-989f-000b6c49d0c2	2017-01-10 19:14:36.633	4771	\N	\N	In all games I've ever played, if a player is all in for less than one big blind, players must still call an amount equal to one big blind and the minimum raise is still to two big blinds as usual.\n\nIf after action has been closed for the betting round, there is one or fewer players who are not all in, then any surplus bets would be returned to the non-allin player.\n\nSo if the blinds are 50/100 but the player in the BB is all in for 25, a player could limp for 100 and if everyone folded then the limper would be returned 100 (75 of their limp and 25 of the SB which the all in player cannot win), leaving a pot of 75 to be won between the limper and the all-in player in the BB.\n\nIn reality when the player in the BB has less than one big blind to bet with, it's very rare that they get heads up anyway, so you just end up with a side pot for the other players.
16179	5	6644	1b17f007-c86a-41c0-84bd-fe2ea4122c2f	2017-01-10 22:06:23.217	3876	\N	added 280 characters in body	I sometimes lose count of the pot size.  At a live game can you ask other players for the size of the pot or is that considered coaching?\r\n\r\nSo far what I have found [here][1]:   \r\n\r\n> Asking for help\r\n> \r\n> You are not allowed to ask anyone at the table for help in playing a\r\n> hand, including the dealer. You may ask what you can bet at any given\r\n> point or general game questions, but you cannot ask what you should do\r\n> in a particular hand. The house rules always state “one player to a\r\n> hand.”\r\n\r\nAnd this [link][2] provided by RoToRa:\r\n\r\n> 50: Pot Size & Pot-Limit Bets   \r\n> A: Players are entitled to a pot count\r\n> in pot-limit only. Dealers will not count the pot in limit and\r\n> no-limit.\r\n\r\nFound [this][3] by chance  \r\n\r\n> The Dealer WILL NOT tell a player the total amount of the pot at any\r\n> time. The dealer may spread the pot for viewing by the player with\r\n> action pending, upon request.\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://learn.pokernews.com/news/2013/11/a-look-at-house-rules-table-talk-2327.htm\r\n  [2]: http://www.pokertda.com/wp-content/uploads/2011/01/Poker-TDA-Rules-2015-Version-1.0-full-longform-PDF-1.pdf\r\n  [3]: https://www.wsop.com/2015/2015-WSOP-tournament-Rules.pdf
