16779	5	8783	b4fae19f-e0c8-4b4f-bfde-35bbcba228a7	2017-03-08 03:52:33.683	3876	\N	added 56 characters in body	It is fair to call it a raise but it would be more proper to call it open for $3 on the flop.  With $3.75 in the pot that is almost a pot size open.  That is a strong call. They have a piece of it (like a Q) or a strong draw.  Since you have flush covered you need to think like JT for an 8 outer straight draw.\r\n\r\nTurn fire again with an almost pot size bet.  I like it. You get another call.  That is a very strong call.  That does not fill in a draw so now you need to put them on a made hand like one pair, two pair, or even a set.\r\n\r\nRiver is a blank.  \r\n\r\nGTO (game theory optimization) dictates bluff a pot size bet 1/3.  Not 1/3 total - 1/3 of the time you bet so 2 value bets and 1 bluff.  \r\n\r\nForget GTO at this point you have to figure you are beat and the only way to win the pot is bluff.  If you bet the pot will you get a fold 1/2 the time? Given two calls you are not likely to get fold with a pot size bet.  If you bet 3x the pot to rep a set or two pair then all you are are only going to get called by is a set or two pair - not a good plan.  If you are going to fire the turn then you need to fire the river but don't over bet - hope they are on a busted draw.  \r\n\r\nI think a better plan is to check the turn. If they bet just get away. Bet the pot on the river not matter what. You have just as much fold equity with 1/3 the investment. It looks like you checked the turn to entice them or you hit the river.\r\n\r\nDeleted as not sure about GTO versus immediate fold equity.
