16832	2	8798	5ad4c749-1768-4f75-91a6-7bf8829be41d	2017-03-13 05:23:56.673	3816	\N	\N	I suspect that due to the fact that there are no community cards makes it difficult to come up with simple shortcuts like in Texas Hold'em. However, after running some quick calculations for heads-up and 3-way play, I did come up with some numbers.\r\n\r\nFor heads-up play, there are 44 unseen cards after 5th street, and 42 cards after 6th street. Multiplying the number of outs by 2.27 will give the probability for making the hand on the river (7th street). Multiplying the number of outs by 4.65 will give the probability for making the hand on the turn (6th street) or river. So the rule of 2 and 4 are fairly close.\r\n\r\nFor 3-way play, where all players stay in the hand to 7th street, there are 41 unseen cards after 5th street, and 38 after 6th street. The numbers change to 2.4 for the turn and 5.1 for the river. In this case, it could be called the rule of 2 and 5.\r\n\r\nWith more players in the game, and the likelihood of many of them folding, the number of unknown cards will vary from hand to hand, so that odds calculations will have to be done on the fly.
16833	2	8799	ab43a622-b069-40e5-b480-1beda5de5999	2017-03-13 06:59:43.37	5050	\N	\N	Generally, the person that has bet last has to show their cards first. So if everyone checks on the river, the person who bet the turn has to show first. In a limped pot that is checked all the way to showdown, the big blind has to show first, since he made the last bet. This "last bet person" is obligated to show their cards. Every other player after that guy doesn't have to show their cards. They can if they want, but they are not obligated to. If they think they are beat, they throw their cards into the muck. This continues clockwise, starting from the first person who showed the cards, until every person has either mucked or shown.
16834	5	8799	8dab145e-fff4-42b7-9fca-6a91f9dbecce	2017-03-13 07:07:58.96	5050	\N	added 293 characters in body; added 25 characters in body	Generally, the person that has bet last has to show their cards first. So if everyone checks on the river, the person who bet the turn has to show first. In a limped pot that is checked all the way to showdown, the big blind has to show first, since he made the last bet. This "last bet person" is obligated to show their cards. Every other player after that guy doesn't have to show their cards. They can if they want, but they are not obligated to. If they think they are beat, they throw their cards into the muck. This continues clockwise, starting from the first person who showed the cards, until every person has either mucked or shown.\n\nThese are the rules in most casinos, the rule who has to show first differ from place to place however. Some casinos (it doesn't occur often) have the rule that the person who is sitting the most left to the dealer has to always show first or that the person that has to act first post flop always has to show first.
16835	5	8794	dd6b46c8-4746-48be-a39a-81f834b108db	2017-03-13 10:31:39.793	1931	\N	added conjecture	This question is about the following theoretical model of poker, which is a greatly simplified version of [my earlier question][1].\r\nSuppose that from one deck of cards, we remove all the Kings, and give one of them to one of the players, whom we'll call the King.\r\nThe other player, whom we'll call Challenger, gets one card randomly from the remaining deck.\r\nOnly Challenger can see this card, so it's not known to the King, but King's card is known to the Challenger.\r\nThus, they both know that the probability of Challenger having a better hand, i.e., possessing an Ace, is 1/12.\r\n\r\nNow they start bidding in the usual way.\r\nIf the blinds are big compared to the stacks, KIng will have an advantage in this game.\r\nBut what happens if the blinds are small?\r\n\r\nAlso, instead of the King, which card would make the above game fair?\r\n\r\nI would also like to formulate a general conjecture: King should never raise, so the game looks like Challenger makes a raise, then King folds or calls.\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: http://poker.stackexchange.com/questions/4458/who-has-advantage-if-one-player-is-known-to-have-an-ace/
16875	2	8814	f9c0d092-1fe4-43c3-9c09-144e12b57df4	2017-03-17 14:06:57.46	3876	\N	\N	Opponents just play AA, KK, QQ, JJ   \r\nThey will win against a random hand 80%  \r\n\r\nYou get a pocket pair 1 in 221 hands  \r\nAgainst 8 other players you will face JJ+ 1 in 7 hands   \r\nSo more than once each orbit    \r\nLet's just trim it down to 1 in 9 to make the math easier     \r\nThe average stack size will be more like 400 as you you will only win like 1 in 4    \r\nSay blinds are 2 and 1   \r\nIn an orbit you will pick up about $24 of blinds    \r\nWhen you go get called you have an EV of -400 + .2(800) = -$240       \r\nYour EV for per orbit is -$216        \r\nYou go broke in 417 hands      \r\n\r\nTable should actually play you a little wider than JJ+ and break you sooner \r\n\r\nPlayer against you has to wait about 6 orbits to hit JJ+  \r\nTheir EV is $222 each 6 orbits    \r\nIn this format going to get like 2 orbits per hour so they are making like $75/hr        \r\n  \r\n\r\n \r\n
16836	5	8795	fb47f690-bb7d-406a-bfcd-02dca5ca4f8b	2017-03-13 10:47:48.77	5050	\N	added 2054 characters in body; added 21 characters in body	The king still has a big edge. Chances of him being beat are 4/51. It is impossible for the challenger to play an unexploitable strategy that is profitable in the long term. \r\n\r\nThe challenger cannot profitably bluff all of his hands, since most of the time he is beat and the king can just call him down. The challenger cannot only bet his value, since the king can just always fold to his bets. A mixture of both strategies will not work, since the challenger, if he is bluffing balanced say with only fives and sixes, will still have way too many hands that are beat by the king. The king can just fold to the bets and still show profit. If the challenger starts bluffing more, the king can start to call everything down and also show profit.\r\n\r\nBluffing al your hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing no hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing a mixture of good and bad hands doesn't work.\r\n\r\nBet sizing will not matter either. Betting exclusivly small with bluffs and value will get the same results as mentioned above. Betting exclusivly big will as well. Betting small with bluffs and big with value is exploitable and the king can only call the challenger's small bets and vice versa. Anything between "small" and "big" will result in the same outcome.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the king will beat the game no mather how big blinds are.\r\n-\r\n\r\nUpdate: additional question is the following: what card would make the game fair?\r\n\r\nThe only card that would make this game a fair game would be an eight instead of a king. Since the only good strategy for the challenger would be betting the same size with value as much of the time you would with bluffs. There are six values higher and lower than an eight, so the challenger is automatically balanced. The game wouldn't be a skill game, since the king is supposed to call all the bets of the challenger because of the price he is getting and the challenger can't do anything besides just betting all his hands. It is just a fifty-fifty coin flip basically.\r\n\r\nA card lower than an eight would be unfair for the king, since if the challenger bets all of his hands, he would have a value hand more often than a bluff. If the king would call all the bets, he would lose, since his opponent is more often than not value betting. If he folds all his hands, he loses his blinds. A mixture of both will obviously still result in losing money.\r\n\r\nA card higher than an eight would be unfair for the challenger, since he can't have enough value to balance it out with bluffs. Say the card is a nine and the challenger plays a balanced strategy. He could value betting a nine plus, that is 6 different cards, so he bluffs the cards 3-8. This is break even, since the king can just toss a coin and decide whether he will call or fold. But the deuce will be checked down and the challenger will lose an extra 4 combinations, therefore the challenger loses money long term. If the challenger decides to include the deuce into his bluffing range, he will bluff too often and the king can call everything down. If the challenger decides to bluff less, the king can just fold all his hands and pick up the blinds when the challenger has a hand he decided to not bluff with.\r\n\r\nConclusion: a fair card would be an eight. This game would be breakeven for both parties, but wouldn't rely on any skill whatsoever. Any card above an eight would be beneficial for the king and any card below an eight would be beneficial for the challenger.\r\n-
16837	5	8795	a02a34da-6e98-47b8-b940-7aa1d909ea3e	2017-03-13 12:29:43.317	5050	\N	deleted 2126 characters in body	The king still has a big edge. Chances of him being beat are 4/51. It is impossible for the challenger to play an unexploitable strategy that is profitable in the long term. \r\n\r\nThe challenger cannot profitably bluff all of his hands, since most of the time he is beat and the king can just call him down. The challenger cannot only bet his value, since the king can just always fold to his bets. A mixture of both strategies will not work, since the challenger, if he is bluffing balanced say with only fives and sixes, will still have way too many hands that are beat by the king. The king can just fold to the bets and still show profit. If the challenger starts bluffing more, the king can start to call everything down and also show profit.\r\n\r\nBluffing al your hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing no hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing a mixture of good and bad hands doesn't work.\r\n\r\nBet sizing will not matter either. Betting exclusivly small with bluffs and value will get the same results as mentioned above. Betting exclusivly big will as well. Betting small with bluffs and big with value is exploitable and the king can only call the challenger's small bets and vice versa. Anything between "small" and "big" will result in the same outcome.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the king will beat the game no mather how big blinds are.\r\n-\r\n
16838	5	8795	f98aca51-9031-46ec-b64c-1b9203442c40	2017-03-13 13:02:00.23	5050	\N	added 1906 characters in body	The king still has a big edge. Chances of him being beat are 4/51. It is impossible for the challenger to play an unexploitable strategy that is profitable in the long term. \r\n\r\nThe challenger cannot profitably bluff all of his hands, since most of the time he is beat and the king can just call him down. The challenger cannot only bet his value, since the king can just always fold to his bets. A mixture of both strategies will not work, since the challenger, if he is bluffing balanced say with only fives and sixes, will still have way too many hands that are beat by the king. The king can just fold to the bets and still show profit. If the challenger starts bluffing more, the king can start to call everything down and also show profit.\r\n\r\nBluffing al your hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing no hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing a mixture of good and bad hands doesn't work.\r\n\r\nBet sizing will not matter either. Betting exclusivly small with bluffs and value will get the same results as mentioned above. Betting exclusivly big will as well. Betting small with bluffs and big with value is exploitable and the king can only call the challenger's small bets and vice versa. Anything between "small" and "big" will result in the same outcome.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the king will beat the game no mather how big blinds are.\r\n-\r\n\r\nWhat card would make this game fair? For the challenger to show profit he has to have one more combination of value in his range than bluffs and his betting range should be more than 50% of all combinations. This way the king has to fold to the challenger's bets, because most of the time the king is beat, meaning the king would lose his blinds more than 50% of the time, thus he would be losing long term.\r\n\r\nThere are a total of 51 combinations possible for the challenger. The challenger thus must bet 26 combinations in order to show profit. 3 combinations can be subtracted, since those are the 'fair card' combinations. What you are left with is 23 combinations that need to be bet, where more than 50% should be value bets. Therefore the challenger can bet 12 value combinations and 11 bluff combinations for him to show the slightest profit possible in this game. If the 'fair card' is a Jack, the challenger can exactly have 12 combinations of value (Aces, Kings and Queens) to show profit. The challenger should look to bluff with 11 combinations and his range is complete. The king can call, which means the challenger has a stronger range and will show profit. The king can fold, but he will fold more than 50% of the time, meaning the challenger will show profit.\r\n\r\nIf the card was a queen, the challenger cannot value bet more than 50% of the time AND bet more than 50% of the time, meaning the king would show profit. The challenger can only bet 8 combinations for value, 3 combinations for a chop, and 7 combinations of bluffs. He needs to bet 26, but can only bet 18.\r\n\r\nSo the fairest card is somewhere in between a Jack or a Queen, but strongly tending towards the Jack, because the challenger is then only winning on 1 combination, while the King would be winning on 8 combinations on a Queen.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the 'fair card' doesn't exist, but the fairest card would be a Jack.\r\n-
