16846	2	8801	e36dae91-f761-49a2-ba25-56c81cb6309f	2017-03-13 21:39:48.007	5050	\N	\N	The assumption that this is a spot he is never bluffing is wrong, he could. You have to look at your river betting range and then decide what to do. Let us look at your river betting range. I think it contains the following hands:\r\n\r\nQJ, J7s and 76s perhaps, 88-TT, T9s, 98s. I am of course not sure about this, but I think this is a reasonable assumption to make.\r\n\r\nAs you can see, you can have full houses that would play exactly the same way as your QJ. Your opponent is representing a boat and you do not block any of your opponent's value range. Besides from J7s and 76s (I doubt it's in your pre-flop raising range in the first place) you are at the bottom of your range. Because of these two things you should be looking to let your hand go. Calling with most/all of your full houses makes sense, since your hand is stronger and it blocks the value hands your opponent might have. This way you are playing an unexploitable balanced strategy.\r\n\r\nConclusion: you are at the bottom of your range and you don't block your opponents value range. Fold.\r\n-
