17320	5	8890	0eb70ec7-814d-4ca0-980b-d12c0a7d1a18	2017-05-04 19:45:04.677	3876	\N	added 188 characters in body	Based on [this][1] it got me thinking. See my answer.\r\n\r\nIf they are playing this 77+, A9s+, KTs+, QTs+, AJo+, KQo top 10% then they only have an 8 12/296 = 4.05%  \r\n\r\nThe chance of no 8 is 1 - (284/296)^4 = .84  \r\n\r\nSo if you remove 10 cards from the deck not 2 and assume .84 of the time the 2 8s are still available then your chance of flopping trips is 12.6% = 1/7.9.  If you take random (50 unknown) then 12.6%.  So odds went up by 10%.\r\n\r\nPot odds 6.9 : 1     \r\n2.5 bb raise and 3 calls = 3 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 / 5 = 4.6 : 1       \r\nStill not getting immediate odds to call    \r\n\r\nA limp and 3 calls = 3 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 / 2 = 5.5 : 1      \r\nStill not getting immediate odds to call     \r\nAt that point cannot assume late callers are play top 10%   \r\n\r\nBack to card removal is real but it does not really matter   \r\n\r\nFound a spot where card removal matters.  Multi way against 4 top 10%  67s, 78s, and 89s has 20% equity and JTs has less 16% equity. Against 3 top 10% 67s, 78s, and 89s has 19% equity.\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://poker.stackexchange.com/questions/410/are-mucked-and-burnt-cards-significant-in-odds-calculation/8886#8886
