18531	2	9314	e8ef3fb5-b26e-40a0-8cac-137536e2be42	2017-09-11 10:45:53.543	3538	\N	\N	In a cash game, yes a player can move seats if a seat is open. Different places might have some different rules, some places might not even allow you to move to a different seat on the same table, but may allow for a table change.\r\n\r\nIn general from what I've seen players can:\r\n\r\n - Move to the button no problem if that seat is open\r\n - Move to the big blind no problem if that seat is open\r\n - I've never seen anyone try to move to the small blind, but I would imagine, and I would do this as a dealer, make them post the bb to.\r\n\r\nIn a tournament, no you cannot move seats, unless the TD moves you as a player, in which case you will have no say on what seat you get.\r\n\r\nIn the case of a tournament and being moved:\r\n\r\n - If you end up on the BB or the button, you get cards and continue as normal\r\n - If you end up as the SB you sit out a hand and there is a dead SB
18532	5	9247	d60677b6-c9a0-4d01-9c2e-1ad9c71ac8a9	2017-09-11 13:48:03.753	3876	\N	added 7 characters in body	Overall it is about twice as hard to make a flush compared to a straight.  \r\n[probability][1]\r\n\r\nYes the last mile might be easier for a flush but the first 4 miles are harder.\r\n\r\nA flush draw has 9 outs (13-4).  An open ended straight draw has 8 outs.   \r\n\r\nAny flush draw is open ended.  You just one of the 4 remaining of the suit.  \r\n\r\nActually on open ended straight filling up on the last card is harder than a flush but that is just a fraction of the straight draws.  You have many more inside straight draws.\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability
