19374	2	9596	c2399e95-893c-480c-8ab1-4df97a7bd781	2018-01-18 11:50:41.683	5810	\N	\N	Novice $1-$2 opponent in Las Vegas. I'm wearing headphones and I have bottom set, bet the flop, bet the turn, bet all in on the river (about $100). Opponent tanks. Finally he shows one pair, throws his cards towards the dealer face up on the table. Dealer scoops his cards, pushes me the pot, I pass my cards in face down and start stacking the pot. Before the cards are dealt, the players next to my opponent decalres that "he said *call*!".\r\n\r\nYou're the floorperson, what do you do?\r\n\r\n - Let's assume he did say call, and other players heard him.\r\n   \r\n   \r\n - The dealer didn't hear him, I didn't hear him. I had the winner, I   \r\n   would have waited for the call.\r\n\r\n   \r\n\r\n - I didn't show anyone my hand.\r\n\r\n   \r\n\r\n - I handed handed in my cards when: I was the only one holding cards,  \r\n   including the dealer, after I was pushed the pot and physically had  \r\n   the chips.\r\n\r\n   \r\n\r\n - Only one player had cards at one point [me].\r\n\r\n   \r\n  \r\n\r\n - Player did say "call", verbal is binding. Only he showed his cards in\r\n   the showdown.\r\n\r\n   \r\n\r\n - Protect your own hand.\r\n\r\n   \r\n\r\n - I physically have the chips. They were pushed to me, and I started   \r\n   stacking them, but the next hand has not started.\r\n\r\nI think one way of looking at it, is that he said call and was the only one who tabled his hand. Another way of looking at is that he didn't say call loud enough for the dealer to hear it, and he didn't protect his hand. However, he did speak up before the next action took place, and it's my responsibility to protect my hand.\r\n
