19689	2	9703	ecf6d7b8-1658-4305-a2d5-78f4724367df	2018-03-02 02:08:34.327	1762	\N	\N	Your right that ruling was wrong.\r\n\r\nA raise is at minimum is double the bet, when someone goes all in for something less then a "full raise" it is not technically a raise. There is not really a good common phrase for what it is. What gets confusing for some people is that these *less then full raises do no reopen the possibility for the original bettor to raise, it also excludes players that have already called the original bet from raising. However it does not exclude players behind the all in from making a raise.\r\n\r\n*(As an aside depending on local rules sometimes if the raise is half the bet or more it reopens the action for the original bettor).\r\n
19690	5	8795	fd32f478-40f0-4770-bc24-0bbc8fe41ea4	2018-03-02 10:12:54.203	4308	\N	The rules state that all the kings are removed, not just the one given to b be King. this leaves only 48 cards in the deck, and means it's impossible for the challenger to be dealt a king. I'm not sure exactly how this changes your math in the paragraph beginning "There are a total of 48 combination	The king still has a big edge. Chances of him being beat are 4/48. It is impossible for the challenger to play an unexploitable strategy that is profitable in the long term. \r\n\r\nThe challenger cannot profitably bluff all of his hands, since most of the time he is beat and the king can just call him down. The challenger cannot only bet his value, since the king can just always fold to his bets. A mixture of both strategies will not work, since the challenger, if he is bluffing balanced say with only fives and sixes, will still have way too many hands that are beat by the king. The king can just fold to the bets and still show profit. If the challenger starts bluffing more, the king can start to call everything down and also show profit.\r\n\r\nBluffing all your hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing no hands doesn't work.\r\nBluffing a mixture of good and bad hands doesn't work.\r\n\r\nBet sizing will not matter either. Betting exclusivly small with bluffs and value will get the same results as mentioned above. Betting exclusivly big will as well. Betting small with bluffs and big with value is exploitable and the king can only call the challenger's small bets and vice versa. Anything between "small" and "big" will result in the same outcome.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the king will beat the game no mather how big blinds are.\r\n-\r\n\r\nWhat card would make this game fair? For the challenger to show profit he has to have one more combination of value in his range than bluffs and his betting range should be more than 50% of all combinations. This way the king has to fold to the challenger's bets, because most of the time the king is beat, meaning the king would lose his blinds more than 50% of the time, thus he would be losing long term.\r\n\r\nThere are a total of 48 combinations possible for the challenger. The challenger thus must bet 23 combinations in order to show profit. What you are left with is 23 combinations that need to be bet, where more than 50% should be value bets. Therefore the challenger can bet 12 value combinations and 11 bluff combinations for him to show the slightest profit possible in this game. If the 'fair card' is a Jack, the challenger can exactly have 12 combinations of value (Aces, Kings and Queens) to show profit. The challenger should look to bluff with 11 combinations and his range is complete. The king can call, which means the challenger has a stronger range and will show profit. The king can fold, but he will fold more than 50% of the time, meaning the challenger will show profit.\r\n\r\nIf the card was a queen, the challenger cannot value bet more than 50% of the time AND bet more than 50% of the time, meaning the king would show profit. The challenger can only bet 8 combinations for value, 3 combinations for a chop, and 7 combinations of bluffs. He needs to bet 26, but can only bet 18.\r\n\r\nSo the fairest card is somewhere in between a Jack or a Queen, but strongly tending towards the Jack, because the challenger is then only winning on 1 combination, while the King would be winning on 8 combinations on a Queen.\r\n\r\nConclusion: the 'fair card' doesn't exist, but the fairest card would be a Jack.\r\n-
