20055	2	9825	b44dda27-1d4c-4d80-99a4-ac689a6058dc	2018-04-17 21:45:53.623	5050	\N	\N	It should never happen that an online tournament has to be aborted. People lose trust and will never come back. You should do everything in your power to prevent such things. However if this happens you can proceed as follows: put the tournament on stop and try to get everything back up asap. If downtime is less than one, maybe two hours this is acceptable. This occurred on pokerstars a couple of years ago. If you cannot fix the problem fast enough you should pay the remaining people out according to their chips and to get some trust back give them some free tournament tickets.
20056	2	9826	3f0257d6-c186-416b-8908-51ace4e98b39	2018-04-18 06:34:46.883	3876	\N	\N	Use [Independent Chip Model (ICM)][1] as that is what it is designed to do.  Convert tournament players stacks in chips into their money equity. You need to use recursion to program it. You could also just use stack size and it won't be far off from ICM.  For sure someone with 1 million chips should get more than 100 chips.\r\n\r\nAt the end of each hand write to disk so you can make things right.\r\n\r\nAs far as human error need to be sure you are playing the game correctly. It is not easy with moving the button, betting, and side pots.  In a tournament need to move tables.  Then you have BOTS - protect against a BOT is some serious coding.\r\n\r\nAs far as real money. You would need to clear that with your local gaming commission. \r\n\r\nI have a REST based server I am working on that I will host in Azure.  If I open source it I will let you know. I plan to NOT take in money for chips.  Play a random table for free.  For a fee can play private games.  If a private game wants to agree to a monetary value of a chip and handle that separately that is up to them. If the server goes down there is a hand history on disk (for a fee). I will let you know when it is active.  I want users on this site to help me test it out.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://www.icmpoker.com/en/blog/poker-icm-101-what-is-icm-poker/
