20065	5	9827	f8136899-bf33-480d-8541-13df8d4500dc	2018-04-18 15:43:08.19	3876	\N	added 102 characters in body	Say you want to play mathematical poker and you want to balance your pre shove range.  A range where your opponent has shown strength.  Either they came in for a raise and you are behind them or they raised you.  Assume you always open for 2.5 BB.  Assume you are medium stack 30 BB just inside the Harrington green zone. \r\n\r\nIf you only shove with QQ+ there are not very many hands (18).  If your opponent knows you only shove with QQ+ then they should only call with KK+.  KK is 50/50 but there will be some overlay money on the table.  Not enough balance to get action from AA.  \r\n\r\nIf you shove with TT+ now you should mathematically get action from QQ+.  Is that enough balance.  So now AA and KK can get action from KK and QQ. \r\n\r\nTT+ feels kind of tight to me and I would add in AKs.  \r\n\r\nWhat I kind of like about a tight raise range is now your call hands can include some strong hands.  \r\n\r\nI know should mix up play. And should mix in bluffs. This a somewhat hypothetical question.\r\n\r\nWhat would be your shove range in that situation?  \r\n\r\nI would like to get the math but just your opinion is good.\r\n\r\nI don't think positions matter here as this is limited to a re-raise and there is no position after an all in.\r\n\r\n 
