20195	2	9875	05067f09-564a-4aea-8d2f-954e9169d147	2018-05-08 21:45:55.1	3876	\N	\N	First need to define bankroll.  It is money dedicated to playing poker.  If you need to to pay rent or buy groceries it is not a bankroll.  \r\n\r\nHave a dedicated bankroll.  If you take out for expenses then it is not longer part of your bankroll.\r\n\r\nIt also depends on what you buy-in for.  Say you buy-in for 100BB versus 200BB.  If you reload if you bust out 100BB then your buy-in is 200BB.  If you are prepared to buy-in 4 times then your buy-in 400BB.  Your buy-in is what you brought to the casino to gamble with.\r\n\r\n100BB 4 times is a little less variance than 400BB once but treat it as 400BB.\r\n\r\nFor cash games it depends a bit on your style.  If you are a maniac you may need 100 buy-in.  If you are conservative then 40 buy-in might be enough.  \r\n\r\nIf you have excess income that can fund your bankroll if you bust out then you can go with a smaller number (even 10).  You may play over you over your target just to bring up (or down) your hourly rate.  You may be a winner at $0.25 $0.50 but play $2 $4 for time management.  After like $2 $4 you should not be jumping for time management (unless your day job pays very well).\r\n\r\nGive yourself a target to drop down.  Say you start at 40 buy-in if you get down to 20 buy-in then drop down.  If you get from 20 buy-in to 10 buy-in then drop down again.  If you have to drop down 3 times then maybe poker is not the game for you.\r\n\r\nIf you started at 40 buy-in then wait for 100 buy-in to jump up. Or rather than jump up take back 40 buy-in as poker profit and stay.  
