5850	101	2018-01-24 16:27:06.857	DukeZhou	2018-01-24 16:27:06.857	http://www.mbrane.rocks	Washington, D.C.	<p>I know almost nothing and am interested in nearly everything.  (This problem is compounded in that I've finally learned just enough to recognize that the more I learn, the more I realize how little I know. <em>I'm working on a function for that</em>;)</p>\n\n<p>Some years ago I made the mistake of taking Joseph Campbell's advice and "following my bliss" <em>(whatever that means lol;)</em> which I don't recommend <em>(if you enjoy creature comforts;)</em> but I'm happy because every day I get to think about a seemingly inexhaustible set of novel, intractable problems.</p>\n\n<p>My greatest weakness is lack of formal mathematical training, and my greatest strength may be not knowing what is easy and what is hard. <em>(This led to the development of a set of non-chance, perfect information, zero-sum, partisan Sudoku games which arise organically from the structure only.)</em>  </p>\n\n<p>I enjoy thinking about how computers do math, and I always favor multiplication over division, and addition most of all because I like whole numbers and care about energy expenditure and heat.  </p>\n\n<p>I'm obsessed with game theoretic conceptions of Sudoku in n dimensions, and optimal decision making in conditions of severely bounded rationality.</p>\n\n<p>My primary interest is in indeterminacy arising from complexity only, how to generate such complexity compactly, and the nature of uncertainty in nature (chaotic systems &amp; quantum stochasticity).</p>\n\n<p>I like to approaching everything starting from first principles <em>(mainly because I'm lazy, but also because it can be fun to re-invent wheels, even wheels that have already been invented. [See: Pierre Menard, Author of the Quixote] Not being purely metaphorical here—I have spent time thinking about how one might go about making actual wheels, just for fun, don't ask me why. Some of you out there know what I'm talking about;)</em></p>\n\n<p>Goals: To describe Sudoku as a "set of sets that are ordered in relation to each other" <em>(so that the constraints are intrinsic as opposed to externally applied.)</em> To understand why I want to do that.</p>\n	0	1	0	https://i.stack.imgur.com/iE1hj.png?s=128&g=1	\N	\N	4146639
